window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["jammu-2023"] = {"id": "jammu-2023", "title": "University of Jammu 2023", "fullTitle": "University of Jammu 2023, M.A. Psychology entrance (real paper)", "sub": "60 real questions · no official key, every answer worked out from standard texts · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social, Health and Organisational Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics", "aptitude": "General Awareness"}, "questions": [{"q": "The field of Psychology crosses boundaries with:", "options": {"a": "Physics", "b": "Philosophy", "c": "Biology", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: psychology crosses boundaries with physics, philosophy and biology, so all of these is correct. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1908) put it neatly: psychology has a long past but only a short history. The short history begins when Wilhelm Wundt (1879) opened his laboratory at Leipzig; the long past lies in three older fields. From philosophy came the first questions. Rene Descartes (1596 to 1650) asked how mind and body interact, and John Locke (An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, 1690) answered that knowledge comes from experience written on a blank slate. From biology and physiology came the body side: Charles Darwin (On the Origin of Species, 1859) made behaviour a product of evolution, Hermann von Helmholtz (1850) timed the nerve impulse, and Wundt called his own textbook Principles of Physiological Psychology (1874). From physics came measurement: Ernst Weber (1834) showed that the just noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the stimulus, and Gustav Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860) turned this into psychophysics, the lawful link between physical energy and sensation. The borders are still open today: neuroscience, brain imaging and the philosophy of mind. Example: when Riya asks how loud a tone must be before she hears it (physics), how her auditory nerve fires (biology) and whether her experience of it matches her friend's (philosophy), she stands on all three borders. Option d is right because each listed field is a genuine parent and present neighbour of psychology. Exam trap: picking the single most famous parent, usually philosophy, when the stem asks about the boundaries psychology crosses.", "topic": "Roots and neighbours of psychology", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychology falls in the domain of:", "options": {"a": "Pure Science", "b": "Pseudo Science", "c": "Information Science", "d": "Positive Science"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: psychology falls in the domain of positive science, the kind of science that describes and explains what is, rather than prescribing what ought to be. Sciences are often sorted into two kinds. A positive science studies facts as they actually are: it observes, measures and experiments, then builds laws that explain and predict events. Physics, chemistry and biology are positive sciences; the label echoes the positivism of Auguste Comte (1798 to 1857), for whom knowledge rests on observed facts. A normative science studies norms or standards, that is, what ought to be: logic sets the norms of correct reasoning, ethics the norms of right conduct and aesthetics the norms of beauty. Psychology asks how people actually perceive, remember, feel and behave. Example: a psychologist studying Arjun's exam anxiety measures his heart rate and test scores and explains when the anxiety rises; whether Arjun ought to cheat to escape it is a question for ethics. Hence the Indian textbook line: psychology is a positive science, not a normative one. Option d is right because psychology describes and explains behaviour as it is, using empirical methods. A pseudo-science only dresses up as science and fails its tests, as astrology and the skull-bump phrenology of Franz Joseph Gall (1758 to 1828) did. Information science studies how information is stored and retrieved (library and computer science). Exam trap: \"pure science\" sounds attractive because psychology has a basic research side, but the textbook contrast being tested is positive versus normative. Key note: psychology does have a pure (basic) side, so \"pure science\" is not absurd; but psychology is equally applied (clinical, counselling, organisational work), and the classic textbook classification is positive science, so d stands.", "topic": "Psychology as positive science", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Psychoanalysis is:", "options": {"a": "Most Controversial School in psychology", "b": "Most unopposed school in psychology", "c": "No More relevant today", "d": "Most relevant today"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: psychoanalysis is the most controversial school in psychology. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) built it from his work with hysterical patients and set it out in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). Its core claims: much of mental life is unconscious, personality forms through childhood psychosexual stages, and the ego protects itself with defence mechanisms such as repression. From the start it drew attack from every side. The philosopher Karl Popper (1902 to 1994) called it unfalsifiable: any behaviour, and its opposite, could be explained after the fact. Behaviourists, starting with John B. Watson (1913), rejected unobservable inner causes such as the unconscious. Critics objected to its stress on sexuality and to theories built on case studies of a narrow, mostly upper-middle-class Viennese sample. Hans Eysenck (1952) questioned whether psychotherapy of this kind worked at all, and Freud's own followers broke away: Alfred Adler in 1911 and Carl Jung in 1913. Yet it is not dead. Unconscious processing is studied in cognitive psychology, defence mechanisms are still taught, and Jonathan Shedler (2010) reviewed evidence that psychodynamic therapy works about as well as other evidence-based therapies. Example: in Meera's class one student calls Freud useless and another says he explains everything; that sharp split is what \"controversial\" means. Option a is right because no other school has been praised and attacked so strongly, for so long, by so many. Exam trap: \"no more relevant\" and \"most relevant\" are absolute claims that each ignore half of the evidence; only \"most controversial\" fits both its lasting influence and its weak scientific support.", "topic": "Psychoanalysis and its critics", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "In Psychology we mostly carry out:", "options": {"a": "Direct Experiments", "b": "Indirect Experiments", "c": "Grounded Theory Studies", "d": "Qualitative Research"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: in psychology we mostly carry out indirect experiments, because the processes we study cannot be observed directly and must be inferred from behaviour. A physicist can put a ruler against a rod, but no one can put a ruler against memory, intelligence, anxiety or an attitude. These are constructs, known only through what they produce. So the psychologist manipulates conditions and measures something observable: reaction time, number of words recalled, errors, a test score, heart rate or skin conductance. The process is then inferred from the pattern. The bridge is the operational definition, a definition of a variable by the operations used to measure it; the physicist Percy Williams Bridgman (1927) proposed operationism and Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought it into psychology. A classic case is Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885): he could not see memory, so he measured the time saved when relearning a list, the savings method. Example: to study Kabir's attention, a researcher counts the targets he misses on a vigilance task and infers how well he sustained attention. Option b is right because nearly every psychological experiment reaches the mind indirectly, through measured behaviour. Exam trap: taking \"direct\" to mean \"done in a laboratory\"; even tightly controlled laboratory experiments are indirect in this sense, because the variable of interest is inferred, not seen. Key note: \"indirect experiment\" is not a standard design label in methods books; the item's point is that psychology studies the mind indirectly, through behaviour, so b stands.", "topic": "Inferring mind from behaviour", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In context of cognitive psychology ERP stands for:", "options": {"a": "Event related Potential", "b": "Event relative Potential", "c": "Early Reaction Potential", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: in cognitive psychology ERP stands for event-related potential. An ERP is the small voltage change in the brain's electrical activity that is time-locked to an event, such as a tone, a word or a decision. The response to one event is tiny and buried in the ongoing EEG, so the EEG is recorded over many trials and averaged; random background activity cancels out and the event-related wave remains. Its peaks are named by polarity (P for positive, N for negative) and latency in milliseconds. The P300, first described by Samuel Sutton and colleagues (1965), appears about 300 milliseconds after a rare target in an oddball task and indexes attention. The N400, found by Marta Kutas and Steven Hillyard (1980), follows a word that does not fit its sentence, such as \"He spread the warm bread with socks\", and indexes meaning. ERPs have excellent timing (millisecond by millisecond) but poor spatial localisation, the reverse of functional MRI. Example: when Arjun hears a rare high tone among many low tones and silently counts the high ones, averaging his EEG across the high tones reveals a large P300 over the parietal scalp. Option a is right because \"event-related potential\" is the standard term. Exam trap: look-alike expansions such as \"event relative\" or \"early reaction\"; and in management, ERP means enterprise resource planning, which has nothing to do with the brain.", "topic": "Event-related potentials", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following demonstrates self-reference effect?", "options": {"a": "Relating information to oneself", "b": "Relating information to others", "c": "Being Self Critical", "d": "Being Egoistic"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: the self-reference effect is the better memory for information that we relate to ourselves. It was shown by Timothy Rogers, Nicholas Kuiper and William Kirker (1977). Participants saw a list of adjectives and made one of four judgements about each: is it printed in big letters (structural), does it rhyme with a given word (phonemic), does it mean the same as a given word (semantic), or does it describe you (self-reference)? In a surprise recall test, words judged against the self were remembered best, about twice as well as words judged for meaning. The result extends the levels of processing view of Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972): deeper, more meaningful encoding leaves more durable traces. The self is a large, well organised and often rehearsed schema, so linking a new item to it gives many retrieval cues at once. Example: Meera learns the defence mechanism projection by recalling the day she blamed her brother for her own bad mood; weeks later she still remembers the term. Option a is right because relating information to oneself is exactly the processing that produces the effect. Relating material to other people helps too, but less; the gap shrinks when the other person is very close, such as one's mother. Exam trap: the effect is a memory phenomenon, not a personality trait; do not confuse it with self-serving bias, egocentrism or self-concept, and do not credit it to Craik and Lockhart, who supplied the framework but not this study.", "topic": "Self-reference effect in memory", "fig": "Timothy Rogers and Nicholas Kuiper"}, {"q": "Which of the following researchers support that emotional tone can influence memory?", "options": {"a": "Hollingworth", "b": "Rychalk", "c": "Thompson", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: all three researchers are associated with the view that emotional tone, how pleasant or unpleasant material feels, influences memory. Harry Levi Hollingworth (1910), in a paper titled The oblivescence of the disagreeable, argued that unpleasant experiences fade from memory faster than pleasant ones, while pleasant memories stay vivid. Experimental studies of feeling tone and recall followed in the 1910s to 1930s, among them R. H. Thomson's (1930) An experimental study of memory as influenced by feeling tone in the Journal of Experimental Psychology. Joseph Rychlak (1977) built the idea into his logical learning theory: people make an affective assessment of everything they meet, judging it liked or disliked, and material they like is learned more readily. Later work kept the theme alive. Margaret Matlin and David Stang (1978) named the Pollyanna principle, the tendency to process pleasant items more accurately and efficiently than unpleasant ones, and Gordon Bower (1981) showed mood-congruent memory: people recall material that matches their current mood. Example: years after a school trip, Kabir remembers the songs on the bus and the waterfall, but has forgotten the rain and the long wait for lunch. Option d is right because each name stands for research showing that the feeling attached to material changes how well it is learned or recalled. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud's (1915) repression is also about forgetting unpleasant material, but it is a clinical theory of motivated forgetting, and it is not offered here. Key note: the printed \"Thompson\" may stand for R. H. Thomson and his 1930 feeling tone study (the spelling differs), but the printed name alone cannot be pinned to one researcher; the item treats all three as researchers on emotional tone and memory, so d stands.", "topic": "Emotional tone and memory", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Creativity is to intelligence as:", "options": {"a": "Sensation is to perception", "b": "Peace is to Law", "c": "Digging the hole deeper is to digging the hole at other place", "d": "Digging the hole at other place is to digging the hole deeper"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is d: creativity is to intelligence as digging the hole at another place is to digging the hole deeper. In the stem pair, creativity and intelligence are two styles of thinking: creativity breaks away from the current approach, while intelligence, as IQ tests measure it, works step by step within it. J. P. Guilford (1950) urged psychologists to study creativity in his APA presidential address, and his structure of intellect model later called the first divergent thinking (many possible answers) and the second convergent thinking (one correct answer). Edward de Bono, who coined the term lateral thinking in The Use of Lateral Thinking (1967), gave the image used in the options; his best-known line on it is \"You cannot dig a hole in a different place by digging the same hole deeper.\" Vertical thinking is logical and sequential; it digs the same hole deeper. Lateral thinking questions the starting assumption and digs somewhere else. So the first term of option d's pair (digging elsewhere) matches creativity, and the second (digging deeper) matches intelligence. The two are related but distinct: by the threshold hypothesis, creativity and IQ correlate only up to an IQ of about 120. Example: Riya's team project keeps failing; Kabir adds more detail to the same plan (digging deeper), while Riya drops the plan and tries a new idea (digging elsewhere). Option d is right because it keeps the same order as the stem: creative, new-direction thinking first, then the logical, same-direction thinking of intelligence. Exam trap: option c contains the very same pair reversed; in analogy items the order of terms matters, and the reversed pair reads intelligence : creativity.", "topic": "Lateral versus vertical thinking", "fig": "Edward de Bono"}, {"q": "The book \"Theories of Personality\" is authored by:", "options": {"a": "Calvin S Hall", "b": "Gardner Lindzey", "c": "John B Campbell", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is d: the book Theories of Personality is authored by Calvin S. Hall, Gardner Lindzey and John B. Campbell, so all of these is correct. Calvin S. Hall (1909 to 1985) and Gardner Lindzey (1920 to 2008) first published Theories of Personality with Wiley in 1957. It became the classic survey of the field: each chapter set out one major theory, from psychoanalysis to self theory, with its structure, dynamics, development, research and evaluation, and generations of students learned personality from it. The book ran through several editions; the fourth (1998) added John B. Campbell as the third author. Both original authors are well known for other work too. Hall studied dreams and, with Robert Van de Castle (1966), devised a system for coding dream content. Lindzey co-authored, from 1951, the Allport, Vernon and Lindzey Study of Values, and in 1959 sorted projective techniques into five families (association, construction, completion, choice or ordering, and expression). Option d is right because all three names appear as authors of the book, Campbell in the later editions. Exam trap: picking only the famous first author; and confusing this book with the many later textbooks by other authors that carry the same title.", "topic": "Hall and Lindzey's textbook", "fig": "Calvin S. Hall and Gardner Lindzey"}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of creative self?", "options": {"a": "Freud", "b": "Adler", "c": "Guilford", "d": "William James"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is b: Alfred Adler gave the concept of the creative self. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911 and founded individual psychology. In his view each person is a goal-seeking whole who strives from feelings of inferiority towards superiority or completeness, guided by an imagined final goal, and pursues it through a unique style of life. What makes the style unique is the creative self, also called the creative power of the self: the active force by which each person interprets heredity and environment and builds a personality from them. Heredity and environment supply the bricks; the person decides how to build the house. This idea, stressed in his later writings, makes Adler's theory less deterministic than Freud's, where instinct and early experience fix the outcome. Example: Kabir and Arjun are brothers raised in the same poor home with the same strict father. Kabir decides the world is unfair and withdraws; Arjun decides hardship is a challenge and works his way into medicine. Same bricks, different houses: that difference is the creative self at work. Option b is right because the term belongs to Adler's individual psychology. Exam trap: the word \"creative\" pulls students towards J. P. Guilford's (1950) research on creativity, and \"self\" towards William James (1890) or Carl Rogers's (1951) self-concept; none of them coined the creative self.", "topic": "Adler's creative self", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "Social & Behavioural Change is a programme associated with:", "options": {"a": "WHO", "b": "APA", "c": "UNICEF", "d": "UNESCO"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Social and Behaviour Change (SBC) is a programme of UNICEF, the United Nations Children's Fund, set up in 1946. SBC is UNICEF's approach to changing the everyday practices and social norms that decide whether children survive and thrive: getting babies vaccinated, washing hands with soap, exclusive breastfeeding, using toilets, keeping girls in school and ending child marriage. It grew out of UNICEF's older Communication for Development (C4D) work, which relied mainly on messages; SBC adds community engagement and the social and behavioural sciences, and UNICEF's Strategic Plan for 2022 to 2025 names it a core change strategy. Much of its toolkit is applied psychology: finding the beliefs, perceived barriers, social norms and cues that drive a behaviour, then designing interventions that target them, rather than simply telling people what to do. Example: a team finds that mothers in a village, like Kavita, skip their babies' second vaccine dose because neighbours say it causes fever; instead of more posters, trusted local women answer questions at the anganwadi and families get reminders, so the belief, the norm and the practical barrier are all addressed. Option c is right because SBC is the name of UNICEF's programme and change strategy. Exam trap: WHO looks tempting because many SBC goals are health goals, and WHO also uses behavioural insights, but the programme named in the stem belongs to UNICEF.", "topic": "UNICEF's behaviour change programme", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The statement \"Students of a school got surprised to see their apparently strict teacher playing with kids in the nearby park\" demonstrates:", "options": {"a": "Social Perception", "b": "Person Perception", "c": "Bias", "d": "Prejudice"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the scene demonstrates person perception, the process by which we form impressions of an individual person's traits, intentions and feelings. We rarely see another person's mind; we infer it from cues such as appearance, voice, nonverbal behaviour, the role the person plays and the setting we meet them in. Solomon Asch (1946) showed how quickly such impressions form and hang together: a stranger described as \"warm\" among a list of traits was judged generous and sociable, while the same list with \"cold\" produced a very different person. Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri (1954) called the beliefs we use to fill the gaps (\"strict people are humourless\") implicit personality theory. Once formed, an impression works like a schema: we expect the person to behave in line with it. Here the students know the teacher only in one role, the classroom, so they have built a narrow impression of a strict man and assumed it describes him everywhere. Seeing him laugh and play with children in a park breaks that expectation, which is why they are surprised and must now revise their picture of him. Option b is right because the stem is about how one particular person is perceived and how that impression collides with new behaviour. Exam trap: social perception sounds equally right, because it is the wider umbrella term; but when a stem centres on impressions of one individual, the specific answer is person perception. Key note: person perception is a part of social perception, so a is not absurd; but the stem is about how one particular person is perceived, and the more specific term wins, so b stands.", "topic": "Person perception and impressions", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"An intelligent person gets recruited in an institute and his colleagues believe him to be the favourite of the recruiter instead of appreciating his skills & expertise\". This scenario is best explained by:", "options": {"a": "Theory of Prejudice", "b": "Attribution Theory", "c": "Actor-observer effect", "d": "Stereotype"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the scenario is best explained by attribution theory, the study of how people explain the causes of behaviour and outcomes. Fritz Heider (The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations, 1958) described everyone as a naive psychologist who asks why things happen and answers with one of two kinds of cause. An internal (dispositional) attribution locates the cause in the person: ability, effort, character. An external (situational) attribution locates it outside: luck, other people, circumstances. Harold Kelley (1967) added the covariation model, in which we weigh consensus, distinctiveness and consistency to decide between the two. In the stem, the new recruit's success could be explained internally (his skill and expertise) or externally (being the recruiter's favourite). His colleagues choose the external cause, perhaps because crediting his ability would make their own standing look weaker. The whole scene is a choice between causal explanations, which is exactly what attribution theory describes. Option b is right because the colleagues are attributing his recruitment to favouritism rather than to his competence. Exam trap: the actor-observer effect (Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett, 1971) sounds close because the colleagues are observers; but that effect says observers lean towards dispositional causes for other people's behaviour, while these observers do the reverse, so the bias does not fit and the broader theory does.", "topic": "Attribution of success", "fig": "Fritz Heider"}, {"q": "\"Two persons see through the same window, one looks towards the sky & the other gazes on the thorns nearby\" exemplifies:", "options": {"a": "Prejudice", "b": "Attitude", "c": "Pessimism", "d": "Hope"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the two persons at the window exemplify attitude, because the same scene is evaluated through two different outlooks. An attitude is a learned, fairly lasting evaluation of an object (a person, thing, idea or situation) along a like-dislike dimension. Gordon Allport (1935) called it \"the most distinctive and indispensable concept in contemporary American social psychology\". The tricomponent or ABC model of Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) gives it three parts: affect (feelings), behaviour (action tendencies) and cognition (beliefs). Attitudes also act like lenses: they steer what we notice and how we interpret it. In the stem both people stand at the same window, so the situation is identical. One looks up at the sky, the hopeful, open view; the other fixes on the thorns, the threatening, negative view. Nothing outside them differs; what differs is the evaluative outlook each brings to the scene, and that is attitude. The line is the stock classroom illustration that our attitude, not our circumstances, shapes what we see. Option b is right because only attitude covers the contrast between the two persons. Exam trap: pessimism and hope are tempting because each fits one of the two people, but the item asks what the pair together shows, the difference in outlook. Key note: pessimism describes only the person looking at the thorns, and hope only the one looking at the sky; the item contrasts the two outlooks, which is attitude, so b stands.", "topic": "Attitude as evaluative outlook", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Aggression can be:", "options": {"a": "Morally Justifiable sometimes", "b": "Morally Unjustifiable", "c": "Seen in action in high pressure games", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: aggression can be morally justifiable sometimes, is usually morally unjustifiable, and is seen in action in high-pressure games, so all of these is correct. Social psychologists define aggression as behaviour aimed at harming another living being who wants to avoid that harm (Robert Baron and Deborah Richardson, Human Aggression, 1994). The definition is about intent and behaviour, not about whether the act is good or bad, which is why all three options hold. First, some aggression is sanctioned or prosocial: force used in self-defence, by a police officer stopping an attacker, or by a parent pulling a child away from a dangerous dog. Society and most moral codes accept such acts. Second, most aggression is morally unjustifiable: bullying, domestic violence, riots and assault harm people for no defensible reason. Third, competitive sport shows aggression in action. Psychologists split aggression by its goal: in hostile aggression harm is the end in itself, hot and impulsive; in instrumental aggression harm is a means to another end, cold and planned. A hard foul to stop a certain goal in a tense final is instrumental; a punch thrown after the whistle is hostile. Example: police constable Kabir pinning down a knife attacker is justified; a senior student hitting Arjun for his lunch money is not; and Riya's deliberately hard tackle to stop a goal in a close football final is instrumental aggression under pressure. Option d is right because each statement describes a real form of aggression. Exam trap: treating aggression as always evil; the definition rests on intent to harm, so justified and unjustified acts both count.", "topic": "Forms and morality of aggression", "fig": ""}, {"q": "\"If a Powerful country invades a poor country based on the opinion of few policy makers only\" it demonstrates concept of:", "options": {"a": "Communalism", "b": "Groupthink", "c": "Crony Capitalism", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: a powerful country invading a poor one on the opinion of a few policy makers demonstrates groupthink. Irving Janis (Victims of Groupthink, 1972; revised 1982) defined groupthink as a mode of thinking in a highly cohesive group in which the drive for agreement overrides a realistic appraisal of the alternatives. Its conditions are a close-knit group, insulation from outside opinion, a directive leader who signals the preferred answer, no method for weighing options, and high stress. Janis listed eight symptoms: an illusion of invulnerability, belief in the group's own morality, collective rationalisation of warnings, stereotyped views of the enemy, self-censorship by doubters, an illusion of unanimity, direct pressure on dissenters, and self-appointed mindguards who keep bad news away. His classic case was the 1961 Bay of Pigs invasion of Cuba, planned by John F. Kennedy's small circle of advisers: experienced, intelligent people who approved a badly flawed plan because nobody wanted to break the consensus. The stem has the same shape: a decision with huge consequences, taken by a few insiders without real debate or outside checks. Janis's remedies follow from the causes: the leader should hold back an opinion, a member should play devil's advocate, and outside experts should be invited to challenge the plan. Option b is right because the scene is a small, insulated decision group reaching a costly consensus. Exam trap: group polarisation (the risky shift, James Stoner, 1961) also concerns group decisions, but it means discussion pushing a group towards a more extreme position, not the suppression of dissent; and the words \"powerful\" and \"poor\" pull students towards political or economic labels that are not group processes.", "topic": "Groupthink in policy decisions", "fig": "Irving Janis"}, {"q": "\"Choosing between working at a toxic workplace or being unemployed\" demonstrates:", "options": {"a": "Approach-Approach Conflict", "b": "Avoidance-Avoidance Conflict", "c": "Frustration", "d": "Approach-Avoidance Conflict"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is b: choosing between working at a toxic workplace and being unemployed is an avoidance-avoidance conflict. Kurt Lewin (1935) described motivational conflicts in terms of valences: a goal with positive valence attracts us (approach) and one with negative valence repels us (avoidance). Crossing the two gives three basic types. In approach-approach conflict a person must choose between two attractive goals, such as two good job offers; it is the easiest to resolve, because either choice is a gain. In avoidance-avoidance conflict a person must choose between two unwanted options, the \"between the devil and the deep sea\" situation. In approach-avoidance conflict a single goal both attracts and repels. Neal Miller (1944) explained the typical behaviour with gradients: the tendency to avoid something grows stronger as one gets closer to it. So in an avoidance-avoidance conflict, moving towards one evil makes it feel worse, the person swings back towards the other, and the result is vacillation, delay and stress; people often try to leave the field altogether, for example by looking for a third option or putting off the decision. In the stem the toxic workplace is unwanted, and unemployment is unwanted too; the person must accept one of them. Option b is right because both alternatives carry negative valence and one must be chosen. Exam trap: the job brings a salary, so students call it approach-avoidance; but that type needs one goal with good and bad sides, while here two separate options are framed as evils. When each of several options has both pros and cons, the type is double approach-avoidance.", "topic": "Lewin's types of conflict", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "In context of communication complete the series Kilo, Lima, Mike, _______.", "options": {"a": "November", "b": "Note Book", "c": "Newspaper", "d": "New Delhi"}, "correct": "a", "section": "aptitude", "explanation": "The answer is a: Kilo, Lima, Mike is followed by November. These are code words from the NATO phonetic alphabet, properly the radiotelephony spelling alphabet of the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO), used in aviation, the armed forces, shipping and radio communication. Each letter has one fixed word, chosen to be understood across languages and over noisy lines: Alfa, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, Golf, Hotel, India, Juliett, Kilo, Lima, Mike, November, Oscar, Papa, Quebec, Romeo, Sierra, Tango, Uniform, Victor, Whiskey, X-ray, Yankee, Zulu. It exists because letters such as B, D, P and V, or M and N, sound alike on a crackling radio. So Kilo, Lima and Mike stand for K, L and M, and the next letter, N, is November. Option a is right because November is the code word for N. Exam trap: all four options begin with N, so the first letter alone does not decide; only November belongs to the code word list.", "topic": "NATO phonetic alphabet", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following genes has relevance with leadership qualities?", "options": {"a": "rs5050", "b": "rs4950", "c": "rs1000", "d": "rs7777"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: the genetic variant rs4950 has been linked with leadership. Jan-Emmanuel De Neve, Slava Mikhaylov, Christopher Dawes, Nicholas Christakis and James Fowler (2013) reported the finding in The Leadership Quarterly in a paper titled \"Born to lead?\". They first used twin data from the US National Longitudinal Study of Adolescent Health to estimate how heritable leadership role occupancy is, that is, whether a person holds a supervisory or managerial position at work. The estimate was about 24 per cent, meaning roughly a quarter of the variation between people in that sample was linked to genes. They then tested specific markers and found that rs4950, a single-nucleotide polymorphism (a one-letter difference in the DNA code) in the CHRNB3 gene, which codes part of a nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, was associated with holding leadership roles, and the association appeared again in a second sample, the Framingham Heart Study. Numbers beginning with \"rs\" are reference SNP identifiers from the dbSNP database, so the other options look plausible but are distractors. The press called rs4950 the first \"leadership gene\", which overstates it: it is one small association, and De Neve himself said the conventional view of leadership as a skill remains largely true, since about three quarters of the variation was not genetic. The finding gives modest support to the old trait or great-person view of leadership, set against situational theories. Option b is right because rs4950 is the marker named in this research. Exam trap: reading \"gene linked to leadership\" as \"gene that makes leaders\"; the study shows a statistical association with role occupancy, not a cause.", "topic": "Genetics of leadership", "fig": "Jan-Emmanuel De Neve"}, {"q": "Which psychologist identified autocratic, democratic & laissez faire leadership types?", "options": {"a": "Kurt Lewin", "b": "Jacob Moreno", "c": "Albert Bandura", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: Kurt Lewin identified the autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire leadership styles. Kurt Lewin (1890 to 1947), often called the father of modern social psychology, ran the classic study with Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939). Adults led after-school clubs of ten-year-old boys making masks and models, and each leader was trained to create one of three social climates. The autocratic leader made every decision and gave orders; the boys worked hard while he watched but stopped when he left the room, and they grew either hostile or apathetic, with more aggression and scapegoating. The democratic leader guided the group to make its own decisions; work was more original, it continued when the leader was absent, and the boys liked this style best. The laissez-faire leader left the boys to themselves; little was done and the group was the most disorganised. The study launched the experimental study of group climates and fed Lewin's work on group dynamics and action research. A common criticism is that it used small samples of American boys. Example: three seniors run Meera's college fest committee. Arjun decides everything himself, Riya talks options through and lets the team vote, and Kabir lets everyone do as they like; Riya's team, like Lewin's democratic groups, keeps working even when she is away. Option a is right because the three styles come from Lewin, Lippitt and White's study. Exam trap: Jacob Moreno (1934) also studied groups and Albert Bandura (1961) also studied children's behaviour, so each sounds possible, but neither proposed these leadership styles; later labels such as transactional and transformational leadership come from other theorists.", "topic": "Lewin's leadership styles", "fig": "Kurt Lewin"}, {"q": "Identify the correct statement:", "options": {"a": "DSM-V™ is published by American Psychiatric Association", "b": "DSM-V™ is published by American Psychological Association", "c": "DSM-5™ is published by American Psychological Association", "d": "DSM-5™ is published by American Psychiatric Association"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: DSM-5 is published by the American Psychiatric Association. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is the American handbook of psychiatric diagnosis: each disorder is defined by explicit criteria (which symptoms, how many, for how long), so that two clinicians seeing the same patient should reach the same label. DSM-I appeared in 1952; DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer, introduced the criteria format; DSM-IV followed in 1994. The fifth edition came out in May 2013 and switched from Roman to Arabic numerals so that later updates could be numbered like software versions (5.1, 5.2); in practice the first major revision was published as DSM-5-TR, the text revision of March 2022. So the correct title is DSM-5, not DSM-V. Option d is right because it pairs the correct numeral with the correct publisher. The other major system, the International Classification of Diseases (ICD), is published by the World Health Organization and is the official system in India and most of the world. Exam trap: two look-alikes are mixed at once. Both the American Psychiatric Association and the American Psychological Association call themselves APA; the psychiatrists' APA publishes the DSM, while the psychologists' APA publishes the Publication Manual (APA referencing style) and the psychologists' ethics code. And 'DSM-V' was the planning-stage name (as in the 2002 'Research Agenda for DSM-V'), but the published book is DSM-5.", "topic": "DSM-5 publisher and naming", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following depicts abnormal behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Seeking too much attention", "b": "Sleeping too much", "c": "SAD", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: excessive attention seeking, sleeping too much and SAD can all depict abnormal behaviour. No single test separates normal from abnormal, so clinicians weigh several criteria together. Statistical rarity calls abnormal whatever is far from the average; violation of social norms calls abnormal whatever breaks a society's rules; maladaptiveness and personal distress ask whether the behaviour harms the person's life or causes suffering. Ronald Comer's Abnormal Psychology textbook condenses these into the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Each option fits once it is persistent and impairing. Seeking too much attention, such as needing to be the centre of attention and feeling uncomfortable when not, is a core feature of histrionic personality disorder. Sleeping too much (hypersomnia) appears in depression, especially with atypical features, and in hypersomnolence disorder. SAD is seasonal affective disorder, depression that returns every autumn or winter and lifts in spring, described by Norman Rosenthal and colleagues in 1984; DSM-5 records it as depression 'with seasonal pattern'. The same abbreviation is also used for social anxiety disorder, which is equally a disorder, so either reading qualifies. Option d is right because each behaviour meets at least one of the four Ds when it is frequent, lasting and disabling. For example, if Kabir sleeps 13 hours a day for months and still cannot get to college, his sleep is deviant and dysfunctional, not just lazy. Exam trap: students pick the single option that sounds most clinical (SAD); in an 'All of these' item, check whether every option can meet the criteria. Key note: none of these is abnormal in a mild or passing form (everyone sleeps in after an exam or wants attention sometimes); the item treats them as clinical signs, so d stands.", "topic": "Criteria of abnormal behaviour", "fig": ""}, {"q": "From biological perspective imbalance in _______ may lead to abnormal behaviour.", "options": {"a": "Neurotransmitters", "b": "Blood Sugar", "c": "Cholesterol", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: from the biological perspective, an imbalance in neurotransmitters may lead to abnormal behaviour. The biological (medical) model holds that mental disorders, like physical illnesses, arise from the body: genes, brain structure and brain chemistry. Neurotransmitters are the chemical messengers that carry signals across the synapse from one neuron to the next, and too much or too little activity in a transmitter system is linked to particular disorders. The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia holds that overactive dopamine pathways produce hallucinations and delusions; it rests on the findings that antipsychotic drugs block dopamine D2 receptors and that amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can cause psychosis. The monoamine hypothesis of depression links low mood to reduced serotonin and norepinephrine activity, which is why selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are prescribed. Low GABA activity is linked to anxiety, and benzodiazepines work by boosting GABA. Modern research sees these as simplified models, but they remain the standard illustration of a biological cause. Option a is right because neurotransmitter imbalance is the textbook example of a biological cause, alongside genes and brain damage, and it guides biological treatment with drugs. For example, if Meera's psychiatrist explains her depression as a serotonin problem and prescribes fluoxetine, he is reasoning from the biological perspective. Exam trap: blood sugar and cholesterol are also bodily, so they look 'biological', but they are general medical markers, not the mechanism the biological model of psychopathology names. Low blood sugar can make someone shaky, confused or irritable, but that is a medical condition affecting behaviour, not a theory of mental disorder.", "topic": "Biological model of abnormality", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The statement \"A person who has been subjected to trauma during childhood may experience OCD\" exemplifies:", "options": {"a": "Psychodynamic Perspective", "b": "Cognitive Perspective", "c": "Life Span Development Perspective", "d": "Social Perspective"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: the statement exemplifies the psychodynamic perspective. The psychodynamic perspective, founded by Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), holds that adult symptoms grow out of early childhood experiences and unresolved unconscious conflicts. Painful experiences are pushed out of awareness by defence mechanisms, yet they keep influencing behaviour and can return in disguised form as symptoms. Freud traced obsessional neurosis (today's obsessive-compulsive disorder, OCD) to conflicts of the anal stage, around ages 1 to 3, over control, dirt and order; in his Rat Man case (1909) a young lawyer's obsessive fears were linked to buried hostility towards his father. On this view, obsessions and compulsions are defences, such as undoing and isolation of affect, that hold anxiety from the childhood conflict in check. Option a is right because the statement explains an adult disorder by a childhood cause, which is the defining logic of the psychodynamic model. Exam trap: the cognitive perspective also explains OCD, but through faulty thinking in the present, not through childhood. Paul Salkovskis (1985) argued that people with OCD feel inflated responsibility for preventing harm, and Stanley Rachman's (1993) thought-action fusion makes them believe that thinking a bad thought is as bad as doing it. A stem that names a thought pattern as the cause points to cognitive; a stem that names a childhood event points to psychodynamic. The social perspective looks to poverty, stigma, culture and support. Key note: a life-span developmental view also links early events to later outcomes, so c is not absurd; but it is a framework for studying change across life rather than a model of the causes of psychopathology, and the classic perspective that explains adult disorder by childhood experience is psychodynamic; a stands.", "topic": "Psychodynamic view of OCD", "fig": "Sigmund Freud"}, {"q": "Specific Phobia in children may be expressed by:", "options": {"a": "Freezing", "b": "Crying", "c": "Tantrums", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: in children, specific phobia may be expressed by freezing, crying and tantrums, so all of these. A specific phobia is a marked fear or anxiety about a particular object or situation, such as dogs, heights, injections, darkness or thunder. Under DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) the object almost always provokes immediate fear; it is actively avoided or endured with intense distress; the fear is out of proportion to the actual danger; and it usually lasts 6 months or more and causes distress or impairment. Adults can usually describe their fear in words, but young children often cannot, so the fear shows in behaviour. DSM-5 therefore adds a note to criterion A: in children, the fear or anxiety may be expressed by crying, tantrums, freezing or clinging. Each option is one of these listed signs. Freezing is the still, rooted response of the fight, flight or freeze system; crying is the plain distress signal; tantrums are explosive protest when the child is pushed towards the feared object. For example, four-year-old Arjun freezes, then screams and throws himself on the floor whenever the neighbour's dog appears; he cannot say 'I am afraid of dogs', but his behaviour says it. Option d is right because DSM-5 names all three expressions (plus clinging), so any single option leaves out the others. Exam trap: DSM-IV required adults to recognise that their fear was excessive but exempted children, and DSM-5 dropped the insight requirement altogether, so a child never needs to say the fear is unreasonable. Do not confuse the child note with the bodily symptoms of a panic attack (palpitations, sweating, trembling).", "topic": "Specific phobia in children", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The risk & prognostic factors of panic disorder may be ______ in nature.", "options": {"a": "Temperamental", "b": "Environmental", "c": "Genetic", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is d: DSM-5 lists temperamental, environmental and genetic (genetic and physiological) risk and prognostic factors for panic disorder, so all of these. Panic disorder means recurrent, unexpected panic attacks (sudden surges of intense fear that peak within minutes, with palpitations, sweating, trembling, breathlessness, dizziness and fear of dying or losing control), followed by at least 1 month of worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour, such as avoiding exercise. For most disorders, DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) groups risk and prognostic factors under three headings, and panic disorder has entries under all of them. Temperamental: negative affectivity (neuroticism) and anxiety sensitivity, the belief that anxiety symptoms are harmful (Steven Reiss and Richard McNally, 1985). Environmental: childhood sexual and physical abuse are reported more often than in some other anxiety disorders, smoking is a risk factor, and most people describe stressors, such as interpersonal problems or illness, in the months before their first attack. Genetic and physiological: many genes each add a little vulnerability, risk is higher in children of parents with anxiety, depressive or bipolar disorders, and breathing problems such as asthma are linked to panic. For example, Meera, a lifelong worrier who smokes and has just lost her job, carries risks under all three headings. Option d is right because each heading appears in the DSM-5 text. Exam trap: students choose 'genetic' because panic runs in families, but DSM-5 lists temperament, environment and biology side by side, a diathesis-stress picture in which they combine, so no single heading is the whole story.", "topic": "Panic disorder risk factors", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Unspecified Psychosis is represented as:", "options": {"a": "F29", "b": "F30", "c": "F45", "d": "F50"}, "correct": "a", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is a: unspecified psychosis is coded F29. Chapter V (F) of the World Health Organization's ICD-10 (1992) gives every mental disorder a code beginning with F, grouped in blocks of up to ten. F00 to F09 are organic disorders such as dementia and delirium; F10 to F19 substance use; F20 to F29 schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders; F30 to F39 mood (affective) disorders; F40 to F48 neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders; F50 to F59 behavioural syndromes with physical factors, starting with eating disorders. The last code in a block is often the 'unspecified' rest category. F20 is schizophrenia and F29 is unspecified nonorganic psychosis, used when a person clearly has psychotic symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech) but there is not enough information for a more specific diagnosis. DSM-5 uses the same code for its 'unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder', because American DSM codes are taken from ICD-10-CM. For example, Kabir is brought to the emergency ward hearing voices, with no history available yet; the psychiatrist may record F29 until the picture becomes clear. Option a is right because F29 closes the psychotic block F20 to F29 as its unspecified category. Exam trap: the neighbouring codes look close. F30 is a manic episode, the first code of the mood block, only one digit away but a different family; F45 is somatoform disorders; F50 is eating disorders such as anorexia nervosa (F50.0) and bulimia nervosa (F50.2). Useful anchors: F20 schizophrenia, F31 bipolar affective disorder, F32 depressive episode, F42 obsessive-compulsive disorder.", "topic": "ICD-10 F code for psychosis", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Cyclothymia is also known as:", "options": {"a": "Bipolar-I Disorder", "b": "Bipolar-II Disorder", "c": "Bipolar-III Disorder", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The answer is c: cyclothymia is informally called bipolar III disorder. Cyclothymic disorder (DSM-5) is a chronic, fluctuating mood disturbance: for at least 2 years (1 year in children and adolescents) the person has numerous periods of hypomanic symptoms and numerous periods of depressive symptoms, but none ever meets the full criteria for a hypomanic, manic or major depressive episode, the symptoms are present at least half the time, and the person is never symptom-free for more than 2 months at a time. The term cyclothymia comes from the German psychiatrists Karl Kahlbaum and Ewald Hecker in the late nineteenth century. Its place in the bipolar family explains the nickname. Bipolar I disorder needs at least one full manic episode; bipolar II disorder needs at least one hypomanic episode plus at least one major depressive episode; cyclothymia is the milder, subthreshold third form, so popular and some clinical writing calls it 'bipolar III'. ICD-10 codes it F34.0, a persistent mood disorder, beside dysthymia (F34.1). For example, Riya has had 3 years of short 'high' spells, when she sleeps little and starts many projects, alternating with flat, low weeks, but never a full episode; this is cyclothymia. Option c is right because cyclothymia is neither bipolar I nor bipolar II, and 'bipolar III' is the name used for it among these choices. Exam trap: students pick bipolar II because both involve hypomanic swings, but bipolar II requires a full major depressive episode, which cyclothymia never reaches. Key note: DSM-5 and ICD never use 'bipolar III', and its meaning varies between bipolar spectrum schemes (in Hagop Akiskal and Olavo Pinto's 1999 scheme it means hypomania brought on by antidepressants), so the alias is informal; but it is the label the item wants, and c stands.", "topic": "Cyclothymic disorder", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which psychological test is used to assess the symptoms of schizophrenia?", "options": {"a": "SAPS", "b": "CAINS", "c": "SANS", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is d: SAPS, CAINS and SANS are all used to assess the symptoms of schizophrenia. Schizophrenia symptoms fall into two broad groups. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience: hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and formal thought disorder. Negative symptoms are losses: flat affect, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of drive), anhedonia and asociality. Rating scales turn a clinical interview into scores, so severity can be tracked over time or compared in research. Nancy Andreasen built two companion scales at the University of Iowa: the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982 to 1983) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The Clinical Assessment Interview for Negative Symptoms (CAINS; Ann Kring, Raquel Gur, Jack Blanchard, William Horan and Steven Reise, 2013) is a newer interview that grew out of a US National Institute of Mental Health consensus that negative symptoms needed better measurement; it rates motivation and pleasure separately from expression. Option d is right because each scale assesses a set of schizophrenia symptoms: SAPS the positive ones, SANS and CAINS the negative ones. For example, a psychologist assessing Arjun might use SAPS to rate his voices and suspiciousness, and SANS or CAINS to rate his flat face and loss of drive. Exam trap: students may think only positive symptoms define schizophrenia, or may not recognise the newer CAINS; all three are schizophrenia instruments. Other well-known ones are the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS; Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein and Lewis Opler, 1987) and the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS; John Overall and Donald Gorham, 1962).", "topic": "Schizophrenia symptom rating scales", "fig": "Nancy Andreasen"}, {"q": "The drug sertraline hydrochloride is used mostly for treatment of:", "options": {"a": "Depression", "b": "Anxiety", "c": "Trauma", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: sertraline is used mostly for treating depression. Sertraline hydrochloride (brand name Zoloft, from Pfizer) is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI). After a neuron releases serotonin into the synapse, a transporter protein (the serotonin transporter, SERT) normally pumps it back in. Sertraline blocks this transporter, so serotonin stays longer in the synapse and keeps acting on the receiving neuron. The mood benefit takes several weeks to appear, even though the blocking happens within hours, which suggests that slower changes in receptors and brain plasticity do the real work. It was approved in the United States in 1991 for major depressive disorder, its first and main indication; later approvals added obsessive-compulsive disorder, panic disorder, post-traumatic stress disorder, social anxiety disorder and premenstrual dysphoric disorder. SSRIs became first-line antidepressants because they are far safer in overdose than the older tricyclics (imipramine) and do not need the diet rules of monoamine oxidase inhibitors. For example, when Meera's psychiatrist diagnoses moderate depression, an SSRI such as sertraline or fluoxetine is a common first prescription. Option a is right because depression is the primary use of sertraline and of the antidepressant class it belongs to. Exam trap: SSRIs are also widely used for anxiety disorders and PTSD, so b and c tempt; the word 'mostly' asks for the main indication. Key note: anxiety disorders and PTSD (trauma) are real, approved uses of sertraline, so b and c are not false uses; but 'mostly' points to its primary indication, depression, and a stands.", "topic": "Sertraline, an SSRI", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is an alternative for Electro convulsive therapy?", "options": {"a": "TMS", "b": "CNS", "c": "PNS", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) is an alternative to electroconvulsive therapy. Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT), introduced by Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini in Rome in 1938, passes a brief electric current through the brain to produce a controlled seizure under general anaesthesia and a muscle relaxant. It is the most effective treatment for severe, treatment-resistant or psychotic depression, but it needs anaesthesia and can cause temporary confusion and memory loss. TMS, introduced by Anthony Barker and colleagues in Sheffield in 1985, places a magnetic coil against the scalp; rapidly changing magnetic pulses pass through the skull and induce small electric currents in the cortex underneath. Repetitive TMS (rTMS) over the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex was cleared by the US Food and Drug Administration in 2008 for depression that had not responded to medication. The patient stays awake, no seizure is induced, no anaesthesia is needed and memory is not affected, so people can return to work the same day. For example, Kabir, whose depression has not lifted after two antidepressants and who fears the memory effects of ECT, may be offered daily rTMS sessions over several weeks. Option a is right because TMS is a brain stimulation treatment for the same main condition, depression, without ECT's seizure and anaesthesia. Exam trap: TMS is an alternative, not an equal; for the most severe or psychotic depression ECT remains more effective. CNS and PNS sound like treatment acronyms, but they are the central and peripheral nervous systems, the parts of the body that treatments act on.", "topic": "TMS as an ECT alternative", "fig": ""}, {"q": "CBT can be used for:", "options": {"a": "Life Style Management", "b": "Addressing Emotional Problems", "c": "Addressing Behavioural Problems", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: CBT can be used for lifestyle management, emotional problems and behavioural problems, so all of these. Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) joins two traditions. Behaviour therapy, built on conditioning research (Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, 1958), changes what people do. Cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron Beck in the 1960s, with Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (1955) as a forerunner, changes how people interpret events. The core model links thoughts, feelings and behaviour: change one and the others shift. Therapy is structured, time-limited and collaborative, with homework, thought records and behavioural experiments. Each option is a recognised use. Emotional problems: CBT is a first-line treatment for depression and the anxiety disorders, including panic, social anxiety and PTSD. Behavioural problems: it treats anger, conduct problems in children (through parent training), substance misuse and habits such as hair pulling. Lifestyle management: CBT for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia, and CBT methods help with weight control, quitting smoking, stress and sticking to treatment in diabetes or heart disease. For example, Arjun uses one CBT programme to challenge the thought 'I will fail' (emotional), stop skipping classes (behavioural) and fix his sleep schedule (lifestyle). Option d is right because CBT's thought, feeling and behaviour model applies to all three areas. Exam trap: students sometimes think CBT is only for mental illness and so drop lifestyle management; health psychology uses CBT widely for health behaviour change, so each single option is incomplete.", "topic": "Applications of CBT", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is correct?", "options": {"a": "To check prediction coefficients both variables should preferably be assessed on basis of same scaling technique.", "b": "To check prediction coefficients variables should preferably not be assessed on basis of same scaling technique.", "c": "Intelligence is mostly assessed using a ratio scale", "d": "Ratio Scale is most common in qualitative enquires"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: to check prediction coefficients, both variables should preferably be measured on the same kind of scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels. Nominal scales only name categories (blood group, religion). Ordinal scales rank (class rank, agreement ratings). Interval scales have equal units but no true zero (temperature in Celsius, IQ). Ratio scales have equal units and a true zero (reaction time, weight, income). Each level permits different statistics. A prediction (correlation or regression) coefficient summarises how well one variable predicts another, and the right coefficient depends on the scale of both variables: Pearson's r assumes both are interval or ratio; Spearman's rho both ranks; phi both true dichotomies; tetrachoric r both artificial dichotomies. So textbooks give the rule of thumb that the two variables should be measured with the same scaling technique. For example, if Riya correlates students' entrance marks with their final marks, both interval, Pearson's r fits; if one teacher only ranks the students, both variables should be ranked and Spearman's rho used. Option a is right because matching the scales lets you use the standard coefficient for that level. Statement c is false because IQ is an interval scale: a score of 0 does not mean zero intelligence, and an IQ of 140 is not twice an IQ of 70. Statement d is false because ratio scales are quantitative, so they are least, not most, common in qualitative enquiry, which works with words and themes. Exam trap: b simply reverses a, a common examiner trick. Key note: mixed-scale coefficients do exist (point-biserial r for one dichotomous and one continuous variable, biserial r, eta), so a is a rule of thumb, not a law; it is the only defensible statement here, so a stands.", "topic": "Scales and correlation coefficients", "fig": ""}, {"q": "SD is to normal probability curve as:", "options": {"a": "Sensitivity & specificity is to ROC curve", "b": "Mean is to Mode", "c": "Median is to Mean", "d": "Correlation is to Regression"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: SD is to the normal probability curve as sensitivity and specificity are to the ROC curve. The relation in the stem pair is 'the measure a curve is built from'. The normal curve is a symmetrical, bell-shaped distribution fully defined by two numbers, its mean and its standard deviation (SD). The SD is the curve's unit of distance: about 68% of cases lie within 1 SD of the mean, about 95% within 2 SD and about 99.7% within 3 SD. Change the SD and the curve becomes taller and narrower or flatter and wider. The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve comes from signal detection theory, first used for radar in the Second World War. A diagnostic test is tried at every possible cut-off score; at each one we compute sensitivity (the true positive rate, the share of real cases the test catches) and specificity (the true negative rate, the share of healthy people it clears). The ROC curve plots sensitivity against 1 minus specificity, and the area under the curve (AUC) summarises accuracy: 0.5 is chance, 1.0 is perfect. For example, when Arjun evaluates a depression screening questionnaire, each cut-off gives one pair of sensitivity and specificity values, and joining the points draws his ROC curve. Option a is right because in both pairs the first term is the quantity the curve is constructed from. Exam trap: b, c and d pair two related statistics with each other (mean and mode, median and mean, correlation and regression), so they feel statistical but lack the measure-to-curve relation. Key note: analogy items are loose, and the SD defines rather than plots the normal curve; but a is the only pair where the first term is what a curve is constructed from, so a stands.", "topic": "SD and ROC curve analogy", "fig": ""}, {"q": "The most famous case study in cognitive psychology is that of:", "options": {"a": "Watson", "b": "Skinner", "c": "Phineas Gage", "d": "Zimbardo"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is c: among the options, the most famous case study is that of Phineas Gage. A case study is an in-depth study of one person, used when an event is too rare or too unethical to create on purpose. In 1848 Phineas Gage, a 25-year-old railway construction foreman in Vermont, was packing blasting powder when an explosion drove his tamping iron, over a metre long, up through his left cheek and out of the top of his skull, destroying much of his left frontal lobe. He survived, could walk and talk, and his memory and intelligence seemed intact. But his personality changed: the reliable, well-liked foreman became impulsive, profane, stubborn and unable to keep to plans. His physician John Martyn Harlow (reports of 1848 and 1868) wrote that friends found him 'no longer Gage'. In 1994 Hanna Damasio and colleagues reconstructed the injury from his skull and placed it in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex. The case became the classic evidence that the frontal lobes govern planning, emotional control, personality and social behaviour. Option c is right because Gage is the only option that is a case study of one person's mind and brain; the other options are researchers. Exam trap: John B. Watson (behaviourism, 1913), B. F. Skinner (operant conditioning, 1938) and Philip Zimbardo (prison study, 1971) are famous for experiments, not for being the subject of a case study; examiners rely on students choosing any familiar name. Key note: in cognitive psychology proper, Henry Molaison (patient H.M.; William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), whose brain surgery left him unable to form new explicit (declarative) long-term memories, is often called the most famous case, and Gage is strictly a neuropsychology case; but H.M. is not offered, so c stands.", "topic": "Phineas Gage case study", "fig": "Phineas Gage"}, {"q": "The reliability of a test is expressed in terms of proportion of:", "options": {"a": "True Score Variance / observed score variance", "b": "Observed Score Variance / true score variance", "c": "True Score Variance × Observed Score Variance", "d": "True Score Variance × error score variance"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The answer is a: the reliability of a test is the proportion true score variance divided by observed score variance. Classical test theory, built on the model Charles Spearman proposed in 1904, says every observed score (X) is a true score (T) plus random error (E): X = T + E. The true score is the average a person would earn over endless independent testings; error is whatever pushes a single score up or down by chance (fatigue, guessing, a noisy room). Because errors are random and uncorrelated with true scores, the variances add: observed variance = true variance + error variance. Reliability is the share of observed-score variance that is true-score variance: true variance / observed variance, which also equals 1 minus error variance / observed variance. It runs from 0 (all error) to 1 (no error). For example, if Meera's anxiety test produces scores with an observed variance of 100, of which 80 is true variance and 20 error, its reliability is 80 / 100 = .80: 80% of the differences between people reflect real differences and 20% are noise. Option a is right because a proportion must put the part (true variance) over the whole (observed variance). Exam trap: b turns the fraction upside down; observed over true can never be below 1 (100 / 80 = 1.25), so it cannot be a proportion. Options c and d multiply variances, and a product is not a share of anything. Test-retest, parallel forms, split-half and Cronbach's alpha are all ways of estimating this same ratio.", "topic": "Reliability as variance ratio", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A child using proper grammar while expressing anything is likely to be in:", "options": {"a": "Sensorimotor Stage", "b": "Preoperational Stage", "c": "Concrete operational Stage", "d": "Formal Operational Stage"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: a child who uses proper grammar in whatever they express is likely to be in the concrete operational stage. Jean Piaget (The Language and Thought of the Child, 1923) described four stages of cognitive development. Sensorimotor (birth to 2): infants know the world through actions; speech moves from babbling to first words around 12 months. Preoperational (2 to 7): the symbolic function appears and vocabulary explodes, but thinking is egocentric and intuitive. Speech shows it: egocentric speech, collective monologues (children 'talking together' without responding to each other) and many grammatical errors, especially overregularisation, applying a rule too widely ('I goed', 'two foots', 'mouses'). Concrete operational (7 to 11): thought becomes logical, decentred and reversible for concrete things; speech becomes socialised, adapted to the listener, and grammar is largely correct and stable. Formal operational (11 onward): abstract, hypothetical reasoning. Complex structures do keep maturing in the school years: Carol Chomsky (1969) showed with a blindfolded doll ('Is the doll easy to see?') that such sentences are still being mastered between ages 5 and 10. For example, five-year-old Kabir says 'I runned fast and catched it'; at nine he says 'I ran fast and caught it' and shapes his story so his listener can follow. Option c is right because consistently correct grammar across all kinds of talk is the school-age, concrete operational picture. Exam trap: language grows fastest in the preoperational stage, so students pick b; but fast growth comes with errors. Key note: much basic grammar is in place by about age 5 to 6, late in the preoperational stage, so b is arguable; but consistently proper grammar in whatever the child says is the concrete operational picture, so c stands.", "topic": "Piaget's stages and grammar", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "A person after retirement from services falls in which of the following conflicts?", "options": {"a": "Approach-Avoidance", "b": "Avoidance-Avoidance", "c": "Integrity vs Despair", "d": "Identity vs Confusion"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: a person who has retired from service faces Erik Erikson's crisis of integrity versus despair. Erik Erikson (Childhood and Society, 1950) described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage poses a crisis, a turning point between two opposite outcomes, and resolving it well yields a virtue. The eighth and last stage, late adulthood (about 60 to 65 onward in most textbooks), is ego integrity versus despair. The person now looks back over the life they have lived. Integrity is accepting that life, failures included, as meaningful and one's own; despair is regret and bitterness that there is no time left to start again, often with fear of death. The virtue is wisdom. Retirement is a classic marker of entry into this stage, because the working role ends and life review begins. For example, after retiring as a bank manager, Rakesh spends his evenings recalling his career and family; if he feels 'I did my best and it mattered', that is integrity; if he feels 'I wasted my years', that is despair. Option c is right because the stem places the person at retirement, which is late adulthood, and that stage's crisis is integrity versus despair. Exam trap: the word 'conflicts' pulls students towards Kurt Lewin's (1935) choice conflicts, approach-avoidance and avoidance-avoidance; Erikson's stages are conflicts too, but between two outcomes of a life stage, and only one option belongs to the life stage of retirement. Key note: retirement can also feel like an approach-avoidance conflict (free time versus lost role and income), so a is not absurd; but the item wants the life-stage crisis, and c stands.", "topic": "Erikson's integrity versus despair", "fig": "Erik Erikson"}, {"q": "Maintaining Social order is a concept central to which of the following theories?", "options": {"a": "Social Identity Theory", "b": "Kohlberg's Theory", "c": "Watson's Theory", "d": "Erickson's Theory"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is b: maintaining social order is central to Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development. Lawrence Kohlberg (doctoral dissertation, University of Chicago, 1958) presented boys with moral dilemmas, the best known being Heinz, who steals a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife. What mattered was not whether a person said Heinz should steal, but the reasoning behind the answer. He found three levels with two stages each. Preconventional: stage 1 obedience and punishment, stage 2 self-interest and exchange. Conventional: stage 3 good boy or good girl (interpersonal approval), and stage 4 the law and order, or social-order-maintaining, orientation. Postconventional: stage 5 social contract and individual rights, stage 6 universal ethical principles. At stage 4, right means doing one's duty, respecting authority and keeping laws, because society would fall apart if everyone broke them. For example, Riya says Heinz must not steal because 'if everyone stole whenever they felt justified, society would collapse'; this is stage 4 reasoning. Option b is right because maintaining the social order names a stage in Kohlberg's sequence, the heart of his conventional level. Exam trap: the words 'social' and 'order' pull towards social identity theory, but Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979) explain how group membership shapes self-esteem and in-group bias, not moral reasoning about laws. Do not confuse stage 4 law and order with stage 5, which sees laws as social contracts that can be changed when they fail to protect rights.", "topic": "Kohlberg's law and order stage", "fig": "Lawrence Kohlberg"}, {"q": "\"A teacher guides a student to have mastery over skills which the student otherwise can't achieve easily\". This statement signifies:", "options": {"a": "Zone of Proximal Development", "b": "Zone of Difficulty", "c": "Zone of Skill Development", "d": "Zone of Personal Development"}, "correct": "a", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is a: the statement describes the zone of proximal development. Lev Vygotsky (writings of the late 1920s and early 1930s; Mind in Society, English edition 1978) held that cognitive development is social: children first do things with other people and only later alone. He defined the zone of proximal development (ZPD) as the distance between what a learner can do independently (the actual developmental level) and what the learner can achieve with guidance from a more knowledgeable other, such as a teacher, parent or skilled peer (the potential level). Teaching works best inside this zone: tasks the learner can already do alone teach little, and tasks far beyond reach cannot be done even with help. 'Proximal' means nearest: the skills that are just about to develop. The support itself was later called scaffolding by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976): the teacher breaks the task into steps, gives hints, models and encourages, and withdraws help as competence grows, like scaffolding taken down once a building can stand. In the stem, the teacher guides the student to master skills the student could not easily reach alone, which is exactly teaching within the ZPD. Option a is right because it is Vygotsky's term for the gap that guidance bridges. Exam trap: the other three options are invented labels built on the same 'Zone of ...' pattern to test whether you know the real term; 'zone of personal development' sounds especially plausible but is not a psychological concept. Do not confuse scaffolding (the help) with the ZPD (the range in which help works), and remember that Jean Piaget (The Origins of Intelligence in Children, 1936), unlike Vygotsky, stressed the child's independent discovery.", "topic": "Vygotsky's zone of proximal development", "fig": "Lev Vygotsky"}, {"q": "Raw Scores are to Z scores as:", "options": {"a": "Covariance is to correlation", "b": "Correlation is to covariance", "c": "Correlation is to regression", "d": "Regression is to correlation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: raw scores are to z scores as covariance is to correlation, because in both pairs the second term is the first one standardised, stripped of its original units. A z score says how far a raw score lies from the mean in standard deviation units: z = (X minus mean) / SD. Every set of z scores has a mean of 0 and an SD of 1, so a raw mark of 60 becomes comparable across tests. Covariance measures how two variables vary together: the average product of each person's deviations from the two means. Its size depends on the units (it changes when centimetres become inches) and it has no fixed upper limit. Karl Pearson (1896) solved this by dividing covariance by the product of the two standard deviations: r = cov(X, Y) / (SD of X times SD of Y). The result always lies between minus 1 and plus 1, whatever the units. Put another way, r is the mean product of paired z scores: covariance computed on z scores. Example: Kabir finds a covariance of 45 between hours of study and marks, which shows direction but not strength; dividing by the two SDs gives r = 0.60, a moderate positive correlation. Option a is right because dividing by the SD turns a raw score into z, and dividing by the two SDs turns covariance into r. Exam trap: option b uses the same two terms in reverse order; in an analogy direction matters, and correlation to covariance runs from the standardised form back to the raw one. Key note: covariance to correlation is the cleaner standardisation, because r is always covariance in z-score units. Option d has a real case, since a raw regression slope standardised in both variables equals r, but only in simple regression with one predictor; with several predictors the standardised weights no longer equal r, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Standardising: z scores and r", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is a goodness of fit test?", "options": {"a": "Chi-Square", "b": "t-test", "c": "ANOVA", "d": "Runs Test"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is a: the chi-square test is the classic goodness of fit test. A goodness of fit test asks how well observed data match the distribution that a hypothesis predicts. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced chi-square for exactly this purpose. You sort cases into categories, count the observed frequency (O) in each, work out the expected frequency (E) if the hypothesis were true, and add up (O minus E) squared divided by E across the categories. A small chi-square means the data fit the expected pattern; a large one, read against the chi-square table with (number of categories minus 1) degrees of freedom, means they do not. Because it works on counts in categories, it needs no normal distribution and is classed as non-parametric. Example: Meera asks 120 students which of four counselling slots they prefer. If there is no preference, each slot should get 30. She observes 50, 30, 25 and 15, and chi-square tells her whether that spread departs from the even split more than chance allows. Applied to a two-way table (are gender and choice of stream related?), the same statistic becomes the chi-square test of independence, a different use. Option a is right because chi-square compares observed counts with the counts a model expects, which is what fit means. Exam trap: the runs test is also non-parametric and also works on counts, so it looks similar, but it tests whether a sequence is random, not whether data fit a distribution. The Kolmogorov-Smirnov one-sample test is the other well-known goodness of fit test, but it is not offered here.", "topic": "Chi-square goodness of fit", "fig": "Karl Pearson"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a correct statement?", "options": {"a": "Mean is the most accurate measure in statistics", "b": "Mean can sometimes mislead a researcher", "c": "Mean is to centre of gravity as median is to mode", "d": "Mean = 3/2 Median"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is b: the mean can sometimes mislead a researcher, because it uses the value of every score and so is pulled by extreme scores and by skew. The arithmetic mean is the sum of the scores divided by their number. Using every score makes it stable from sample to sample and the basis of most parametric statistics, but it also lets one outlier drag it away from where most scores lie. The scores 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8. Add a single 40 and the mean jumps to 13.3, higher than five of the six scores, while the median moves only from 7 to 8. In skewed data such as income the mean runs into the long tail: if a businessman earning crores joins a room of ten clerks, the 'average income' in the room becomes huge, though no clerk is any richer. That is why median income, not mean income, is usually reported. Example: Arjun reports that patients at his clinic waited 'on average 40 minutes', but most waited 10 to 15 minutes and two waited four hours. The median gives the truer picture. Option b is right because the mean describes the centre well only when the data are roughly symmetrical and free of outliers. Exam trap: option a sounds like textbook praise, since the mean is the most stable and most widely used average, but no measure is the most accurate in every case. The right choice depends on the scale of measurement and the shape of the distribution.", "topic": "Limits of the mean", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a parametric test?", "options": {"a": "Unpaired t-test", "b": "ANOVA", "c": "Kruskal Wallis Test", "d": "Paired t-test"}, "correct": "c", "section": "research", "explanation": "The answer is c: the Kruskal-Wallis test is not parametric; it is a non-parametric, rank-based test. Parametric tests estimate population parameters such as the mean, and assume interval or ratio scores that are roughly normally distributed in the population, with similar variances across groups. Non-parametric (distribution-free) tests drop the normality assumption; most turn the scores into ranks and compare the ranks. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) devised the H test as a one-way ANOVA by ranks. All scores from three or more independent groups are pooled and ranked, and H checks whether the average rank differs between the groups, read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom. Each common parametric test has a rank partner: unpaired t-test and Mann-Whitney U (two independent groups); paired t-test and Wilcoxon signed-rank (two related samples); one-way ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis (three or more independent groups); repeated-measures ANOVA and Friedman (three or more related samples). Example: Kabir compares pain ratings on a 1 to 10 scale across three hospital wards. The ratings are ordinal and badly skewed, so he uses Kruskal-Wallis instead of ANOVA. Option c is right because Kruskal-Wallis works on ranks and makes no assumption of a normal population. Exam trap: the paired t-test is often mistaken for a non-parametric test because it handles related samples, and ANOVA because it compares several groups; both still assume normal, interval data. The non-parametric partner of the paired t-test is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test.", "topic": "Parametric vs non-parametric tests", "fig": "William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis"}, {"q": "If a child is able to ask you about your wellbeing he is likely to have achieved _______ to some extent.", "options": {"a": "Social Development", "b": "Emotional Development", "c": "Socio-emotional Development", "d": "Socio-cultural Development"}, "correct": "c", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is c: a child who asks how you are has reached some socio-emotional development, because the act joins an emotional skill with a social one. Socio-emotional (psychosocial) development is the domain of change in emotions, self-understanding and relationships, beside the physical and cognitive domains. To ask 'Are you feeling better?' a child must first read or imagine another person's feelings, an emotional capacity built on empathy. Martin Hoffman (Empathy and Moral Development, 2000) traced empathy from global empathy in infancy (a baby cries when another cries) to concern for another's feelings from about age 2 to 3. The child also needs a theory of mind, the understanding that others have inner states that can differ from one's own. David Premack and Guy Woodruff (1978) coined the term, and Heinz Wimmer and Josef Perner (1983) devised the false-belief task, which most children pass from about age 4 to 5. Finally the child must turn that understanding into a caring social act: starting a conversation, showing concern, waiting for the answer. Example: four-year-old Anaya sees her grandmother lying down and asks, 'Nani, does your head hurt?' Option c is right because the behaviour needs both emotional understanding and social skill, and socio-emotional development is the term that covers the two together. Exam trap: social development and emotional development each describe only half of the act, and socio-cultural development sounds close but refers to Lev Vygotsky's theory (Mind in Society, English edition 1978) that culture and social interaction shape thinking.", "topic": "Socio-emotional development", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Which of the following is not usually covered in a health belief model?", "options": {"a": "Perceived Susceptibility", "b": "Perceived Severity", "c": "Perceived Benefits", "d": "Perceived Social Support"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: perceived social support is not part of the health belief model. The health belief model (HBM) was built in the 1950s by social psychologists in the US Public Health Service, Godfrey Hochbaum, Irwin Rosenstock and Stephen Kegeles, to explain a puzzle: why so few people came for free tuberculosis chest X-rays. Rosenstock (1966) gave it its classic statement. It says a person takes a health action when four beliefs line up: perceived susceptibility (I could get this), perceived severity (it would be serious), perceived benefits (the action would help) and perceived barriers (cost, pain, time, fear), which must be outweighed by the benefits. A cue to action, such as a symptom, a reminder or a friend's illness, triggers the step, and in 1988 Rosenstock, Victor Strecher and Marshall Becker added self-efficacy, the belief that one can carry the action out. Example: Rohan, a 45-year-old smoker, believes he could get lung cancer, that it would be deadly and that a screening scan helps, but the clinic is far away. When his uncle is diagnosed (a cue to action), he books the scan. Option d is right because social support, the sense of being cared for and helped by others, belongs to other accounts of health, such as the stress-buffering hypothesis of Sheldon Cohen and Thomas Wills (1985), and is not a component of the model. Exam trap: all four options begin with 'perceived', so the odd one hides by copying the pattern. Remember the core four as susceptibility, severity, benefits and barriers.", "topic": "Health belief model components", "fig": "Irwin Rosenstock"}, {"q": "Which Country tops the list of happy countries in the world as per World Happiness Report-2022?", "options": {"a": "Norway", "b": "Finland", "c": "France", "d": "USA"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is b: Finland topped the World Happiness Report 2022, its fifth year in a row at number one. The World Happiness Report is an annual survey-based ranking, first issued in 2012 and published for many years by the UN Sustainable Development Solutions Network; its editors have included John Helliwell, Richard Layard, Jeffrey Sachs and Jan-Emmanuel De Neve. It ranks countries mainly by life evaluations from the Gallup World Poll: people rate their lives on the Cantril ladder (Hadley Cantril, 1965) from 0, the worst possible life, to 10, the best, and the report uses a three-year average. It then explains the differences with six factors: GDP per capita, social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, generosity and perceived corruption. In the 2022 report Finland was first, Denmark second and Iceland third, with Norway eighth. The Nordic countries score high because of strong social support, trust in institutions, low corruption and secure welfare systems, not because of sunshine or sheer wealth. Option b is right because Finland held first place in every report from 2018 to 2022, and the 2022 edition was its fifth consecutive win. Exam trap: Norway is the classic distractor. It topped the 2017 report and is a Nordic neighbour, so a student who remembers only 'a Scandinavian country' can pick the wrong one.", "topic": "World Happiness Report 2022", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Hans Selye, the proponent of general adaptation syndrome, had core specialization in the field of:", "options": {"a": "Endocrinology", "b": "Psychology", "c": "Biopsychology", "d": "Health Psychology"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Hans Selye's core field was endocrinology, the medical science of hormones and the glands that release them. Hans Selye (1907 to 1982) was born in Vienna, trained in medicine and chemistry in Prague, and did his life's work in Canada, at McGill University and then the University of Montreal. While hunting for a new sex hormone, he injected rats with ovarian and placental extracts and found the same changes whatever he injected; cold, injury and toxic drugs produced them too. In a 1936 letter to Nature, 'A syndrome produced by diverse nocuous agents', he described the triad: enlarged adrenal cortex, shrunken thymus and lymph nodes, and stomach ulcers. He called this nonspecific response the general adaptation syndrome, with three stages, alarm, resistance and exhaustion, and popularised it in The Stress of Life (1956), later separating eustress (good stress) from distress. His evidence was hormonal throughout: the pituitary and adrenal glands carried the response. Option a is right because Selye was a physician and endocrinologist who studied glands and hormones in laboratory animals; he was never trained as a psychologist. Exam trap: because the general adaptation syndrome appears in every health psychology and biopsychology chapter, students assume Selye belonged to those fields. Health psychology only became an organised field in the late 1970s, decades after his discovery.", "topic": "Selye and endocrinology", "fig": "Hans Selye"}, {"q": "Which of the following exemplifies health enhancing behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Taking a Balanced Diet", "b": "Wearing a Seat Belt", "c": "Taking Multivitamins", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: a balanced diet, wearing a seat belt and taking multivitamins are all taught as examples of health-enhancing behaviour. Stanislav Kasl and Sidney Cobb (1966) defined health behaviour as any action taken by a person who believes himself healthy, to prevent illness or detect it while there are no symptoms. They set it apart from illness behaviour (what a person does on noticing a symptom) and sick-role behaviour (what they do, once they regard themselves as ill, to get well). Health psychology texts then sort such habits into those that build fitness and wellbeing and those that guard against illness and injury. A balanced diet is the model health-enhancing habit: it supports energy and healthy weight and lowers the risk of heart disease and diabetes. Wearing a seat belt is the textbook safety habit: a small, cheap act that sharply cuts the risk of death and serious injury in a crash. Taking multivitamins is a classic preventive habit people adopt against deficiency, and for some (a pregnant woman taking folic acid, an older person short of vitamin D) it clearly helps. Example: Priya eats dal, vegetables and fruit daily, buckles up in every cab and takes the vitamin D her doctor advised. Option d is right because each act is a voluntary step taken while healthy to promote or protect health. Exam trap: students pick a alone because diet sounds like the only 'enhancing' habit; the item uses the term broadly, for any health-promoting behaviour. Key note: seat belts are more exactly called health-protective, and evidence for routine multivitamins in well-nourished adults is weak; the item groups all three as health-promoting behaviours, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Health-enhancing behaviours", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Who proposed the concept of positive organizational behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Fred Luthans", "b": "Taylor", "c": "Fayol", "d": "Weber"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: Fred Luthans proposed the concept of positive organisational behaviour. Fred Luthans (2002), a management professor at the University of Nebraska, carried Martin Seligman's positive psychology movement (launched during Seligman's 1998 APA presidency) into the workplace. He defined positive organisational behaviour (POB) as the study and application of positively oriented human resource strengths and psychological capacities that can be measured, developed and effectively managed for better performance. His tests for including a capacity were strict: it must rest on theory and research, be measurable, be state-like (open to development through training, unlike a fixed trait) and relate to performance. The four capacities that best met these tests became psychological capital (PsyCap), remembered as HERO: hope, efficacy (confidence, from Albert Bandura's self-efficacy, 1977), resilience and optimism. Luthans, Carolyn Youssef and Bruce Avolio set this out in Psychological Capital (2007). Example: Neha, a bank branch manager in Pune, runs short workshops in which tellers set goals, plan routes around obstacles and rehearse bouncing back after an angry customer, all aimed at raising their hope, efficacy and resilience. Option a is right because both the concept and the term POB come from Luthans. Exam trap: the other three are founders of classical management and organisation theory, so all are 'organisation' names. None of them wrote about positive psychological capacities; their concern was efficiency, administration and formal structure.", "topic": "Positive organisational behaviour", "fig": "Fred Luthans"}, {"q": "As per which therapy people are responsible for their behaviour?", "options": {"a": "Reality Therapy", "b": "REBT", "c": "CBT", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: reality therapy holds that people are responsible for their own behaviour. William Glasser (1925 to 2013), an American psychiatrist, developed it while working with delinquent girls at the Ventura School and published Reality Therapy in 1965. Its later base, choice theory (Choice Theory, 1998), says that nearly all behaviour is chosen to meet five built-in needs: survival, love and belonging, power (achievement), freedom and fun. Because behaviour is chosen, the person is responsible for it, not the past, the parents or a 'mental illness', and can learn to choose better. Glasser even spoke of 'depressing' rather than 'being depressed'. The therapist is warm but firm, focuses on present behaviour, accepts no excuses and uses no punishment. Robert Wubbolding (1980s) summed up the method as WDEP: Wants (what do you want?), Doing and direction (what are you doing?), Evaluation (is it working?) and Planning (a simple, specific plan). Example: Aditya, 16, blames his teachers for failing. The therapist asks what he wants (to pass), what he is doing (skipping classes) and whether it is working (no), then helps him plan to attend every class this week. Option a is right because personal responsibility for present, chosen behaviour is the central principle of reality therapy. Exam trap: REBT and CBT also ask clients to take ownership of their thinking, so they look plausible, but their central idea is that irrational or distorted beliefs cause distress. Key note: Albert Ellis's REBT (1955) and Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1960s) do hold people responsible for their beliefs, so b and c are not absurd; but responsibility for behaviour is the defining principle of reality therapy, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Reality therapy and responsibility", "fig": "William Glasser"}, {"q": "\"Psychologists establish relationships of trust with those with whom they work\". This statement highlights the principle of:", "options": {"a": "Fidelity & Responsibility", "b": "Justice", "c": "Integrity", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: the statement is the opening line of Principle B, Fidelity and Responsibility, in the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016). The code has two layers: five aspirational General Principles (A to E) that guide judgement but are not enforced, and ten sections of enforceable Ethical Standards. The five principles are: A, Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (benefit clients and do no harm); B, Fidelity and Responsibility (build trust, keep commitments, accept responsibility for one's conduct, manage conflicts of interest and care about colleagues' ethical conduct); C, Integrity (accuracy, honesty and truthfulness, with no stealing, cheating or fraud); D, Justice (fairness and equal access to the benefits of psychology); and E, Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (privacy, confidentiality and self-determination). Fidelity means faithfulness: keeping promises and being someone others can rely on. Example: Sneha, a school counsellor, promises a teenage client that she will tell him before sharing anything with his parents, and keeps that promise; that is fidelity. Declining to become his private tutor as well, because a second role could exploit or confuse the relationship, is responsibility. Option a is right because 'establish relationships of trust' is the defining phrase of fidelity in the code. Exam trap: integrity also sounds like trustworthiness, but in the APA code it means honesty and accuracy in science, teaching and practice. Trust within working relationships belongs to fidelity.", "topic": "APA General Principles", "fig": ""}, {"q": "A counsellor must have ________ for the client.", "options": {"a": "Empathy", "b": "Genuineness", "c": "Positive Regard", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is d: a counsellor must offer the client empathy, genuineness and positive regard, the three core conditions of person-centred therapy. In 'The necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change', Carl Rogers (1957) listed six conditions; the three that describe the therapist became the core conditions. Congruence or genuineness: the counsellor is real and transparent, with no professional front, so what they say matches what they feel; Rogers later called it the most basic. Unconditional positive regard: warm acceptance of the client as a person, without conditions of worth, even when the counsellor does not approve of every act; the term was coined by his student Stanley Standal (1954). Accurate empathic understanding: sensing the client's inner world 'as if' it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality, and communicating that understanding back. Rogers held that when a client experiences these conditions, the client's own actualising tendency does the rest. Example: Farhan tells his counsellor he copied in an exam. She does not lecture him (positive regard), reflects 'you feel ashamed, and also trapped by the pressure' (empathy), and admits honestly when she has not yet understood something (genuineness). Option d is right because each of the three is a required condition, and the options list all of them. Exam trap: students pick empathy alone because it is the best-known condition, but in Rogers's view all three must be present together.", "topic": "Rogers's core conditions", "fig": "Carl Rogers"}, {"q": "The technique mainly encompassed in family therapy is:", "options": {"a": "Interpersonal Therapy", "b": "Intrapersonal Therapy", "c": "Group Therapy", "d": "None of these"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is a: family therapy is mainly interpersonal, since it locates problems in the relationships between family members rather than only inside one person. Family therapy grew in the 1950s from systems thinking: Ludwig von Bertalanffy (1901 to 1972) argued in his general systems theory that a change in one part of a system changes the whole. Clinicians noticed that a patient who improved in hospital often relapsed at home, and that when one member got better another sometimes fell ill, so the family became the client. The member brought for help is the 'identified patient', a sign of a troubled system rather than its cause. The work is on interaction: Murray Bowen's family systems therapy (Family Therapy in Clinical Practice, 1978: differentiation of self, triangles), Salvador Minuchin's structural therapy (Families and Family Therapy, 1974: boundaries, subsystems), Jay Haley's strategic therapy (Problem-Solving Therapy, 1976: directives) and Virginia Satir's communication approach (Conjoint Family Therapy, 1964) all aim to change communication, roles, boundaries and alliances. Example: Ishaan, 12, refuses to go to school. The therapist sees that he and his mother are enmeshed while his father is left out, and works on the parents' alliance and the boundary around the child. Option a is right because family therapy works between people (interpersonal), not within one person's mind. Exam trap: group therapy also has several people in the room, so it looks right, but group members are usually strangers with no shared life outside the group. Key note: some texts call family therapy a special form of group therapy, and Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984) is a separate individual treatment for depression; the item contrasts an interpersonal focus with an intrapersonal one, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Family therapy as interpersonal work", "fig": ""}, {"q": "Frank Parsons, the proponent of vocational counselling, was educated as:", "options": {"a": "Doctor", "b": "Engineer", "c": "Psychologist", "d": "Neuroscientist"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is b: Frank Parsons was educated as an engineer. Frank Parsons (1854 to 1908) entered Cornell University at 15 and graduated in civil engineering. He took a railway engineering post in Massachusetts, but lost it when the firm failed in the Panic of 1873. He then worked as a teacher, studied law and passed the Massachusetts bar (1881), and became a writer, lecturer and social reformer in Boston. Working with young people at the Civic Service House, he set up the Vocation Bureau of Boston in 1908, the first organised vocational guidance service. His book Choosing a Vocation appeared in 1909, after his death, and earned him the title father of vocational guidance. The book set out the trait-and-factor approach in three steps: a clear understanding of yourself (aptitudes, abilities, interests, limits), knowledge of the requirements and prospects of different kinds of work, and 'true reasoning' on the relation between the two. This matching idea runs through later career theory, including John Holland's person-environment fit (RIASEC, 1959 onwards). Option b is right because Parsons's formal training was in engineering, not in medicine or psychology; psychology as a profession barely existed when he was a student. Exam trap: because Parsons is a founding figure of counselling, students assume he was a psychologist. He was an engineer turned lawyer and social reformer.", "topic": "Frank Parsons's training", "fig": "Frank Parsons"}, {"q": "Who proposed the ERG theory of motivation?", "options": {"a": "Alderfer", "b": "Maslow", "c": "Weber", "d": "Goleman"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is a: Clayton Alderfer proposed the ERG theory of motivation. Clayton Alderfer (1969; book Existence, Relatedness, and Growth, 1972) reworked Abraham Maslow's (1943) five-level hierarchy into three groups of needs. Existence needs are material and physical (food, water, pay, safe working conditions), matching Maslow's physiological and safety levels. Relatedness needs are for meaningful relationships with family, colleagues and friends, matching belongingness and the social part of esteem. Growth needs are for personal development and full use of one's abilities, matching self-esteem and self-actualisation. ERG differs from Maslow in three ways: it was tested on people at work, several needs can be active at the same time, and it adds frustration-regression, meaning that when a higher need is blocked a person falls back on a lower one and seeks it more strongly. Example: Sunil, a software engineer, is passed over for a promotion that would have let him lead a new project (growth blocked). He starts pushing hard for a pay rise and spends more time with his friends on the team (existence and relatedness). Option a is right because ERG, with its three needs and frustration-regression, is Alderfer's theory. Exam trap: Maslow is the natural bait because ERG is a compressed version of his hierarchy. But Maslow's model is taught as a ladder climbed from the bottom up, without frustration-regression; the three-letter ERG label belongs to Alderfer.", "topic": "Alderfer's ERG theory", "fig": "Clayton Alderfer"}, {"q": "Who among the following is not an organizational Scientist?", "options": {"a": "Taylor", "b": "Fayol", "c": "Weber", "d": "Ulric Neisser"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: Ulric Neisser is not an organisational scientist; he was a cognitive psychologist. Ulric Neisser (1928 to 2012) named and shaped the field of cognitive psychology in his book Cognitive Psychology (1967), which drew together research on attention, pattern recognition and memory and gave us the terms iconic and echoic memory. In Cognition and Reality (1976) he proposed the perceptual cycle and pressed for ecologically valid research on memory in everyday life. None of this concerns how organisations are structured or managed. The other three are the founders of classical organisation theory. Frederick Winslow Taylor (The Principles of Scientific Management, 1911) used time-and-motion study to find the 'one best way' to do each job and tied pay to output. Henri Fayol (Administration Industrielle et Générale, 1916) described management from the top down: five functions (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling) and 14 principles such as unity of command and the scalar chain. Max Weber (1864 to 1920) described bureaucracy as the rational ideal of organisation: a hierarchy of offices, written rules, specialised roles, impersonal treatment and appointment on merit. Option d is right because Neisser's work lies in cognition, while Taylor, Fayol and Weber are the classical organisation theorists. Exam trap: Weber was a sociologist, not a manager or consultant, so students sometimes pick him as the odd one out; but his model of bureaucracy is a cornerstone of organisation theory.", "topic": "Classical organisation theorists", "fig": "Ulric Neisser"}, {"q": "The book \"Job Satisfaction: From Assessment to Intervention\" is authored by:", "options": {"a": "Herzberg", "b": "Maslow", "c": "Paul Spector", "d": "Martin Seligman"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: Job Satisfaction: From Assessment to Intervention (Routledge, 2022) was written by Paul Spector. Paul Spector, an industrial and organisational psychologist and Distinguished Professor Emeritus at the University of South Florida, is a leading researcher on job satisfaction. He defines it simply as the extent to which people like or dislike their jobs, overall or facet by facet. In 1985 he published the Job Satisfaction Survey (JSS), a 36-item scale measuring nine facets: pay, promotion, supervision, fringe benefits, contingent rewards, operating procedures, co-workers, nature of work and communication. His book Job Satisfaction: Application, Assessment, Causes, and Consequences (1997) became a standard reference, and the 2022 book updates it. Its chapters cover the nature and assessment of satisfaction, how people feel about work, causes in the job and in the person (including personality and person-job fit), links with behaviour, performance and physical and mental health, and interventions to raise satisfaction. Option c is right because Spector is the author of this title, which grew out of his earlier book on the same subject. Exam trap: Frederick Herzberg is the name students link most with job satisfaction, through his two-factor theory, so he is the strongest bait; but his famous book is The Motivation to Work (1959), and he died in 2000, long before this title appeared.", "topic": "Spector and job satisfaction", "fig": "Paul Spector"}, {"q": "The coach of a cricket team is to ______ leadership as _______ is to charismatic leadership.", "options": {"a": "Charismatic, Politician", "b": "Transformational, Manager", "c": "Transactional, Inspirational Leader", "d": "Autocratic, Charisma"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: the coach of a cricket team is to transactional leadership as an inspirational leader is to charismatic leadership. James MacGregor Burns (Leadership, 1978) separated transactional leadership from transforming leadership, which Bernard Bass (1985) developed into the transformational model. Transactional leadership is an exchange: the leader sets clear targets and roles, rewards good performance (contingent reward) and corrects or sanctions poor performance (management by exception). A cricket coach fits the picture: fitness targets, selection as the reward for runs and wickets, being dropped for poor form. Charismatic leadership goes back to Max Weber's charismatic authority (Economy and Society, 1922), obedience to a person seen as extraordinary, and became a modern theory with Robert House (1977): the charismatic leader states an inspiring vision, shows great confidence and makes followers identify with the leader and the cause. An inspirational leader, such as Mahatma Gandhi leading the freedom movement, is the stock example. So the first blank pairs a role with the style that suits it (coach with transactional), and the second names the kind of person who embodies the given style (inspirational leader with charismatic). Option c is right because it is the only option in which both halves fit. Exam trap: option a tempts because politicians are often charismatic, but it makes the coach charismatic and leaves the analogy with no contrast between the two halves. Key note: many great coaches are also transformational or inspiring, so the analogy is loose; but only c gives a first term that suits a coach and a second that matches charismatic leadership, so the answer used here stands.", "topic": "Transactional and charismatic leadership", "fig": ""}, {"q": "In terms of structure, organizations can be:", "options": {"a": "Flat", "b": "Tall", "c": "Matrix Type", "d": "All of these"}, "correct": "d", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is d: organisations can be flat, tall or matrix in structure, so all three options are correct. Organisational structure is the formal pattern of jobs, reporting lines and authority, usually drawn as an organisation chart. Two features shape it: the number of levels in the hierarchy and the span of control, the number of people who report to one manager. A tall structure has many levels and a narrow span of control. It gives close supervision and clear promotion steps, but decisions are slow and messages get distorted as they pass up and down; armies and government departments are classic tall structures. A flat structure has few levels and a wide span of control. Decisions are faster, staff have more autonomy and communication is easy, but each manager carries a heavy load; start-ups and small firms are usually flat. A matrix structure lays project teams across functional departments, so each employee has two bosses, a functional manager (say, engineering) and a project manager (say, a new metro line). It pools expertise across projects but can cause conflict over priorities, and is common in engineering, consulting and aerospace firms. Example: Kavya, a designer at an engineering firm in Bengaluru, reports both to the head of design and to the manager of the bridge project she is placed on. Option d is right because each listed type is a recognised organisational structure. Exam trap: tall and flat are often taught as a pair, so students forget that the matrix design is a structure too. Other recognised forms include functional, divisional and network structures.", "topic": "Organisational structures", "fig": ""}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"jammu-2023": [{"q": "The field of Psychology crosses boundaries with:", "concept": "The answer is d: psychology crosses boundaries with physics, philosophy and biology, so all of these is correct. Hermann Ebbinghaus (1908) put it neatly: psychology has a long past but only a short history. The short history begins when Wilhelm Wundt (1879) opened his laboratory at Leipzig; the long past lies in three older fields. From philosophy came the first questions. Rene Descartes (1596 to 1650) asked how mind and body interact, and John Locke (An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, 1690) answered that knowledge comes from experience written on a blank slate. From biology and physiology came the body side: Charles Darwin (On the Origin of Species, 1859) made behaviour a product of evolution, Hermann von Helmholtz (1850) timed the nerve impulse, and Wundt called his own textbook Principles of Physiological Psychology (1874). From physics came measurement: Ernst Weber (1834) showed that the just noticeable difference is a constant fraction of the stimulus, and Gustav Fechner (Elements of Psychophysics, 1860) turned this into psychophysics, the lawful link between physical energy and sensation. The borders are still open today: neuroscience, brain imaging and the philosophy of mind. Example: when Riya asks how loud a tone must be before she hears it (physics), how her auditory nerve fires (biology) and whether her experience of it matches her friend's (philosophy), she stands on all three borders. Option d is right because each listed field is a genuine parent and present neighbour of psychology. Exam trap: picking the single most famous parent, usually philosophy, when the stem asks about the boundaries psychology crosses.", "traps": {"a": "Physics is a real border through psychophysics (Ernst Weber and Gustav Fechner measuring how sensation grows with stimulus energy), but it is only one of the three fields listed.", "b": "Philosophy is the oldest parent (the mind-body problem, John Locke's empiricism), but choosing it alone ignores psychology's biological and physical roots.", "c": "Biology is a real border (Charles Darwin's evolution, physiology, today's neuroscience), but alone it leaves out philosophy and physics, which shaped psychology just as much."}}, {"q": "Psychology falls in the domain of:", "concept": "The answer is d: psychology falls in the domain of positive science, the kind of science that describes and explains what is, rather than prescribing what ought to be. Sciences are often sorted into two kinds. A positive science studies facts as they actually are: it observes, measures and experiments, then builds laws that explain and predict events. Physics, chemistry and biology are positive sciences; the label echoes the positivism of Auguste Comte (1798 to 1857), for whom knowledge rests on observed facts. A normative science studies norms or standards, that is, what ought to be: logic sets the norms of correct reasoning, ethics the norms of right conduct and aesthetics the norms of beauty. Psychology asks how people actually perceive, remember, feel and behave. Example: a psychologist studying Arjun's exam anxiety measures his heart rate and test scores and explains when the anxiety rises; whether Arjun ought to cheat to escape it is a question for ethics. Hence the Indian textbook line: psychology is a positive science, not a normative one. Option d is right because psychology describes and explains behaviour as it is, using empirical methods. A pseudo-science only dresses up as science and fails its tests, as astrology and the skull-bump phrenology of Franz Joseph Gall (1758 to 1828) did. Information science studies how information is stored and retrieved (library and computer science). Exam trap: \"pure science\" sounds attractive because psychology has a basic research side, but the textbook contrast being tested is positive versus normative. Key note: psychology does have a pure (basic) side, so \"pure science\" is not absurd; but psychology is equally applied (clinical, counselling, organisational work), and the classic textbook classification is positive science, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Pure (basic) science seeks knowledge for its own sake; psychology does have such a side, but it is just as much an applied science, so this label describes only half of it.", "b": "A pseudo-science (astrology, Franz Joseph Gall's phrenology) only looks scientific and fails empirical tests; psychology tests its claims by observation and experiment.", "c": "Information science studies how information is stored, organised and retrieved (library and computer science); it is a separate discipline, not the domain psychology belongs to."}}, {"q": "Psychoanalysis is:", "concept": "The answer is a: psychoanalysis is the most controversial school in psychology. Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) built it from his work with hysterical patients and set it out in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). Its core claims: much of mental life is unconscious, personality forms through childhood psychosexual stages, and the ego protects itself with defence mechanisms such as repression. From the start it drew attack from every side. The philosopher Karl Popper (1902 to 1994) called it unfalsifiable: any behaviour, and its opposite, could be explained after the fact. Behaviourists, starting with John B. Watson (1913), rejected unobservable inner causes such as the unconscious. Critics objected to its stress on sexuality and to theories built on case studies of a narrow, mostly upper-middle-class Viennese sample. Hans Eysenck (1952) questioned whether psychotherapy of this kind worked at all, and Freud's own followers broke away: Alfred Adler in 1911 and Carl Jung in 1913. Yet it is not dead. Unconscious processing is studied in cognitive psychology, defence mechanisms are still taught, and Jonathan Shedler (2010) reviewed evidence that psychodynamic therapy works about as well as other evidence-based therapies. Example: in Meera's class one student calls Freud useless and another says he explains everything; that sharp split is what \"controversial\" means. Option a is right because no other school has been praised and attacked so strongly, for so long, by so many. Exam trap: \"no more relevant\" and \"most relevant\" are absolute claims that each ignore half of the evidence; only \"most controversial\" fits both its lasting influence and its weak scientific support.", "traps": {"b": "The opposite of the truth: psychoanalysis was opposed from its start by behaviourists, experimental psychologists, philosophers of science such as Karl Popper and even its own dissenters, Carl Jung and Alfred Adler.", "c": "Overstated: many of Freud's specific theories lack support, but unconscious processing, defence mechanisms and psychodynamic therapy remain in research and practice.", "d": "Also overstated: today's mainstream is cognitive, biological and cognitive-behavioural; psychoanalysis is one influence among many, not the most relevant school."}}, {"q": "In Psychology we mostly carry out:", "concept": "The answer is b: in psychology we mostly carry out indirect experiments, because the processes we study cannot be observed directly and must be inferred from behaviour. A physicist can put a ruler against a rod, but no one can put a ruler against memory, intelligence, anxiety or an attitude. These are constructs, known only through what they produce. So the psychologist manipulates conditions and measures something observable: reaction time, number of words recalled, errors, a test score, heart rate or skin conductance. The process is then inferred from the pattern. The bridge is the operational definition, a definition of a variable by the operations used to measure it; the physicist Percy Williams Bridgman (1927) proposed operationism and Stanley Smith Stevens (1935) brought it into psychology. A classic case is Hermann Ebbinghaus (1885): he could not see memory, so he measured the time saved when relearning a list, the savings method. Example: to study Kabir's attention, a researcher counts the targets he misses on a vigilance task and infers how well he sustained attention. Option b is right because nearly every psychological experiment reaches the mind indirectly, through measured behaviour. Exam trap: taking \"direct\" to mean \"done in a laboratory\"; even tightly controlled laboratory experiments are indirect in this sense, because the variable of interest is inferred, not seen. Key note: \"indirect experiment\" is not a standard design label in methods books; the item's point is that psychology studies the mind indirectly, through behaviour, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "A direct experiment would observe the variable itself, as a physicist measures a rod's length; mental processes such as memory or anxiety are never seen directly, only inferred from responses.", "c": "Grounded theory (Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss, 1967) is a specific qualitative method that builds theory from interview data; it is used, but it is not what psychologists mostly do.", "d": "Qualitative research (interviews, thematic analysis) is a real and growing approach, but mainstream psychology is mostly quantitative and experimental."}}, {"q": "In context of cognitive psychology ERP stands for:", "concept": "The answer is a: in cognitive psychology ERP stands for event-related potential. An ERP is the small voltage change in the brain's electrical activity that is time-locked to an event, such as a tone, a word or a decision. The response to one event is tiny and buried in the ongoing EEG, so the EEG is recorded over many trials and averaged; random background activity cancels out and the event-related wave remains. Its peaks are named by polarity (P for positive, N for negative) and latency in milliseconds. The P300, first described by Samuel Sutton and colleagues (1965), appears about 300 milliseconds after a rare target in an oddball task and indexes attention. The N400, found by Marta Kutas and Steven Hillyard (1980), follows a word that does not fit its sentence, such as \"He spread the warm bread with socks\", and indexes meaning. ERPs have excellent timing (millisecond by millisecond) but poor spatial localisation, the reverse of functional MRI. Example: when Arjun hears a rare high tone among many low tones and silently counts the high ones, averaging his EEG across the high tones reveals a large P300 over the parietal scalp. Option a is right because \"event-related potential\" is the standard term. Exam trap: look-alike expansions such as \"event relative\" or \"early reaction\"; and in management, ERP means enterprise resource planning, which has nothing to do with the brain.", "traps": {"b": "\"Event relative potential\" is a garbled form of the real term; the potential is related (time-locked) to an event, and no method carries this name.", "c": "\"Early reaction potential\" is invented; early ERP components exist (such as the N100), but ERP is not short for this phrase.", "d": "Wrong because option a gives the standard expansion used in cognitive neuroscience."}}, {"q": "Which of the following demonstrates self-reference effect?", "concept": "The answer is a: the self-reference effect is the better memory for information that we relate to ourselves. It was shown by Timothy Rogers, Nicholas Kuiper and William Kirker (1977). Participants saw a list of adjectives and made one of four judgements about each: is it printed in big letters (structural), does it rhyme with a given word (phonemic), does it mean the same as a given word (semantic), or does it describe you (self-reference)? In a surprise recall test, words judged against the self were remembered best, about twice as well as words judged for meaning. The result extends the levels of processing view of Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972): deeper, more meaningful encoding leaves more durable traces. The self is a large, well organised and often rehearsed schema, so linking a new item to it gives many retrieval cues at once. Example: Meera learns the defence mechanism projection by recalling the day she blamed her brother for her own bad mood; weeks later she still remembers the term. Option a is right because relating information to oneself is exactly the processing that produces the effect. Relating material to other people helps too, but less; the gap shrinks when the other person is very close, such as one's mother. Exam trap: the effect is a memory phenomenon, not a personality trait; do not confuse it with self-serving bias, egocentrism or self-concept, and do not credit it to Craik and Lockhart, who supplied the framework but not this study.", "traps": {"b": "Relating information to other people (other-reference) also deepens encoding, but it gives a smaller memory boost than relating it to the self, so it is the comparison condition, not the effect.", "c": "Being self-critical is a personality or emotional style (linked to perfectionism and low self-esteem), not a way of encoding information for memory.", "d": "Egoism is a trait or ethical stance of putting self-interest first; the self-reference effect concerns memory and happens in everyone, whatever their selfishness."}}, {"q": "Which of the following researchers support that emotional tone can influence memory?", "concept": "The answer is d: all three researchers are associated with the view that emotional tone, how pleasant or unpleasant material feels, influences memory. Harry Levi Hollingworth (1910), in a paper titled The oblivescence of the disagreeable, argued that unpleasant experiences fade from memory faster than pleasant ones, while pleasant memories stay vivid. Experimental studies of feeling tone and recall followed in the 1910s to 1930s, among them R. H. Thomson's (1930) An experimental study of memory as influenced by feeling tone in the Journal of Experimental Psychology. Joseph Rychlak (1977) built the idea into his logical learning theory: people make an affective assessment of everything they meet, judging it liked or disliked, and material they like is learned more readily. Later work kept the theme alive. Margaret Matlin and David Stang (1978) named the Pollyanna principle, the tendency to process pleasant items more accurately and efficiently than unpleasant ones, and Gordon Bower (1981) showed mood-congruent memory: people recall material that matches their current mood. Example: years after a school trip, Kabir remembers the songs on the bus and the waterfall, but has forgotten the rain and the long wait for lunch. Option d is right because each name stands for research showing that the feeling attached to material changes how well it is learned or recalled. Exam trap: Sigmund Freud's (1915) repression is also about forgetting unpleasant material, but it is a clinical theory of motivated forgetting, and it is not offered here. Key note: the printed \"Thompson\" may stand for R. H. Thomson and his 1930 feeling tone study (the spelling differs), but the printed name alone cannot be pinned to one researcher; the item treats all three as researchers on emotional tone and memory, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Harry Levi Hollingworth (1910) is one of the three: he argued that disagreeable experiences are forgotten faster; choosing him alone leaves out the other two names.", "b": "\"Rychalk\" is Joseph Rychlak, whose logical learning theory holds that liked material is learned better; he is correct, but only one of the three.", "c": "\"Thompson\" (possibly R. H. Thomson, whose 1930 study tested memory and feeling tone) also belongs to this research, so picking it alone leaves out Hollingworth and Rychlak."}}, {"q": "Creativity is to intelligence as:", "concept": "The answer is d: creativity is to intelligence as digging the hole at another place is to digging the hole deeper. In the stem pair, creativity and intelligence are two styles of thinking: creativity breaks away from the current approach, while intelligence, as IQ tests measure it, works step by step within it. J. P. Guilford (1950) urged psychologists to study creativity in his APA presidential address, and his structure of intellect model later called the first divergent thinking (many possible answers) and the second convergent thinking (one correct answer). Edward de Bono, who coined the term lateral thinking in The Use of Lateral Thinking (1967), gave the image used in the options; his best-known line on it is \"You cannot dig a hole in a different place by digging the same hole deeper.\" Vertical thinking is logical and sequential; it digs the same hole deeper. Lateral thinking questions the starting assumption and digs somewhere else. So the first term of option d's pair (digging elsewhere) matches creativity, and the second (digging deeper) matches intelligence. The two are related but distinct: by the threshold hypothesis, creativity and IQ correlate only up to an IQ of about 120. Example: Riya's team project keeps failing; Kabir adds more detail to the same plan (digging deeper), while Riya drops the plan and tries a new idea (digging elsewhere). Option d is right because it keeps the same order as the stem: creative, new-direction thinking first, then the logical, same-direction thinking of intelligence. Exam trap: option c contains the very same pair reversed; in analogy items the order of terms matters, and the reversed pair reads intelligence : creativity.", "traps": {"a": "Sensation is to perception is raw input to its interpretation, one stage feeding the next; creativity and intelligence are two styles of thinking, not two stages of one process.", "b": "Peace and law are a social state and the rules that may protect it; nothing in this pair mirrors new-direction versus same-direction thinking.", "c": "The right pair in the wrong order: digging deeper is the vertical, intelligence-like thinking and digging elsewhere the creative kind, so this pair reads intelligence : creativity."}}, {"q": "The book \"Theories of Personality\" is authored by:", "concept": "The answer is d: the book Theories of Personality is authored by Calvin S. Hall, Gardner Lindzey and John B. Campbell, so all of these is correct. Calvin S. Hall (1909 to 1985) and Gardner Lindzey (1920 to 2008) first published Theories of Personality with Wiley in 1957. It became the classic survey of the field: each chapter set out one major theory, from psychoanalysis to self theory, with its structure, dynamics, development, research and evaluation, and generations of students learned personality from it. The book ran through several editions; the fourth (1998) added John B. Campbell as the third author. Both original authors are well known for other work too. Hall studied dreams and, with Robert Van de Castle (1966), devised a system for coding dream content. Lindzey co-authored, from 1951, the Allport, Vernon and Lindzey Study of Values, and in 1959 sorted projective techniques into five families (association, construction, completion, choice or ordering, and expression). Option d is right because all three names appear as authors of the book, Campbell in the later editions. Exam trap: picking only the famous first author; and confusing this book with the many later textbooks by other authors that carry the same title.", "traps": {"a": "Calvin S. Hall is the first author, but not the only one; Gardner Lindzey co-wrote it from 1957 and John B. Campbell joined later.", "b": "Gardner Lindzey is a co-author from the first edition (1957), but the book also carries Hall's name and, from 1998, Campbell's.", "c": "John B. Campbell joined only with the fourth edition (1998); he is an author, but choosing him alone leaves out the two original authors."}}, {"q": "Who gave the concept of creative self?", "concept": "The answer is b: Alfred Adler gave the concept of the creative self. Alfred Adler (1870 to 1937) broke with Sigmund Freud in 1911 and founded individual psychology. In his view each person is a goal-seeking whole who strives from feelings of inferiority towards superiority or completeness, guided by an imagined final goal, and pursues it through a unique style of life. What makes the style unique is the creative self, also called the creative power of the self: the active force by which each person interprets heredity and environment and builds a personality from them. Heredity and environment supply the bricks; the person decides how to build the house. This idea, stressed in his later writings, makes Adler's theory less deterministic than Freud's, where instinct and early experience fix the outcome. Example: Kabir and Arjun are brothers raised in the same poor home with the same strict father. Kabir decides the world is unfair and withdraws; Arjun decides hardship is a challenge and works his way into medicine. Same bricks, different houses: that difference is the creative self at work. Option b is right because the term belongs to Adler's individual psychology. Exam trap: the word \"creative\" pulls students towards J. P. Guilford's (1950) research on creativity, and \"self\" towards William James (1890) or Carl Rogers's (1951) self-concept; none of them coined the creative self.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud described personality as id, ego and superego (The Ego and the Id, 1923), driven by instincts and early experience; he had no creative self, and Adler's concept was partly a reply to his determinism.", "c": "J. P. Guilford studied creativity as divergent thinking within his structure of intellect model; that is a cognitive ability, not Adler's personality force that shapes a style of life.", "d": "William James (Principles of Psychology, 1890) split the self into the I (knower) and the Me (known) with material, social and spiritual selves; he did not propose a creative self."}}, {"q": "Social & Behavioural Change is a programme associated with:", "concept": "The answer is c: Social and Behaviour Change (SBC) is a programme of UNICEF, the United Nations Children's Fund, set up in 1946. SBC is UNICEF's approach to changing the everyday practices and social norms that decide whether children survive and thrive: getting babies vaccinated, washing hands with soap, exclusive breastfeeding, using toilets, keeping girls in school and ending child marriage. It grew out of UNICEF's older Communication for Development (C4D) work, which relied mainly on messages; SBC adds community engagement and the social and behavioural sciences, and UNICEF's Strategic Plan for 2022 to 2025 names it a core change strategy. Much of its toolkit is applied psychology: finding the beliefs, perceived barriers, social norms and cues that drive a behaviour, then designing interventions that target them, rather than simply telling people what to do. Example: a team finds that mothers in a village, like Kavita, skip their babies' second vaccine dose because neighbours say it causes fever; instead of more posters, trusted local women answer questions at the anganwadi and families get reminders, so the belief, the norm and the practical barrier are all addressed. Option c is right because SBC is the name of UNICEF's programme and change strategy. Exam trap: WHO looks tempting because many SBC goals are health goals, and WHO also uses behavioural insights, but the programme named in the stem belongs to UNICEF.", "traps": {"a": "WHO (World Health Organization) is the UN's health agency that sets global health guidance; it works on health behaviour too, but the Social and Behaviour Change programme is UNICEF's.", "b": "APA (the American Psychological Association) is a professional body of psychologists known for its journals, publication manual and ethics code; it does not run this UN field programme.", "d": "UNESCO is the UN agency for education, science and culture (literacy, heritage, science policy); this child-focused behaviour change programme is not its programme."}}, {"q": "The statement \"Students of a school got surprised to see their apparently strict teacher playing with kids in the nearby park\" demonstrates:", "concept": "The answer is b: the scene demonstrates person perception, the process by which we form impressions of an individual person's traits, intentions and feelings. We rarely see another person's mind; we infer it from cues such as appearance, voice, nonverbal behaviour, the role the person plays and the setting we meet them in. Solomon Asch (1946) showed how quickly such impressions form and hang together: a stranger described as \"warm\" among a list of traits was judged generous and sociable, while the same list with \"cold\" produced a very different person. Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri (1954) called the beliefs we use to fill the gaps (\"strict people are humourless\") implicit personality theory. Once formed, an impression works like a schema: we expect the person to behave in line with it. Here the students know the teacher only in one role, the classroom, so they have built a narrow impression of a strict man and assumed it describes him everywhere. Seeing him laugh and play with children in a park breaks that expectation, which is why they are surprised and must now revise their picture of him. Option b is right because the stem is about how one particular person is perceived and how that impression collides with new behaviour. Exam trap: social perception sounds equally right, because it is the wider umbrella term; but when a stem centres on impressions of one individual, the specific answer is person perception. Key note: person perception is a part of social perception, so a is not absurd; but the stem is about how one particular person is perceived, and the more specific term wins, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Social perception is the broad study of how we perceive people, groups and social situations, including ourselves; it contains person perception, but the stem centres on an impression of one individual.", "c": "A bias is a systematic error in judgement (such as the confirmation bias); the students' first impression was limited, but the stem describes forming and revising an impression, not a named bias.", "d": "Prejudice is a negative attitude toward people because of their group membership (Gordon Allport, 1954); the stem involves no group and no hostility, only an impression of one teacher."}}, {"q": "\"An intelligent person gets recruited in an institute and his colleagues believe him to be the favourite of the recruiter instead of appreciating his skills & expertise\". This scenario is best explained by:", "concept": "The answer is b: the scenario is best explained by attribution theory, the study of how people explain the causes of behaviour and outcomes. Fritz Heider (The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations, 1958) described everyone as a naive psychologist who asks why things happen and answers with one of two kinds of cause. An internal (dispositional) attribution locates the cause in the person: ability, effort, character. An external (situational) attribution locates it outside: luck, other people, circumstances. Harold Kelley (1967) added the covariation model, in which we weigh consensus, distinctiveness and consistency to decide between the two. In the stem, the new recruit's success could be explained internally (his skill and expertise) or externally (being the recruiter's favourite). His colleagues choose the external cause, perhaps because crediting his ability would make their own standing look weaker. The whole scene is a choice between causal explanations, which is exactly what attribution theory describes. Option b is right because the colleagues are attributing his recruitment to favouritism rather than to his competence. Exam trap: the actor-observer effect (Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett, 1971) sounds close because the colleagues are observers; but that effect says observers lean towards dispositional causes for other people's behaviour, while these observers do the reverse, so the bias does not fit and the broader theory does.", "traps": {"a": "Prejudice is a negative attitude toward members of a group (Gordon Allport, The Nature of Prejudice, 1954); the colleagues are explaining one man's success, and no group membership drives their judgement.", "c": "The actor-observer effect (Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett, 1971) predicts that observers blame or credit another's disposition; here the observers pick a situational cause, the opposite of the effect.", "d": "A stereotype is a generalised belief about the members of a group (Walter Lippmann's \"pictures in our heads\", 1922); the stem describes explaining one person's success, not a belief about a group."}}, {"q": "\"Two persons see through the same window, one looks towards the sky & the other gazes on the thorns nearby\" exemplifies:", "concept": "The answer is b: the two persons at the window exemplify attitude, because the same scene is evaluated through two different outlooks. An attitude is a learned, fairly lasting evaluation of an object (a person, thing, idea or situation) along a like-dislike dimension. Gordon Allport (1935) called it \"the most distinctive and indispensable concept in contemporary American social psychology\". The tricomponent or ABC model of Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960) gives it three parts: affect (feelings), behaviour (action tendencies) and cognition (beliefs). Attitudes also act like lenses: they steer what we notice and how we interpret it. In the stem both people stand at the same window, so the situation is identical. One looks up at the sky, the hopeful, open view; the other fixes on the thorns, the threatening, negative view. Nothing outside them differs; what differs is the evaluative outlook each brings to the scene, and that is attitude. The line is the stock classroom illustration that our attitude, not our circumstances, shapes what we see. Option b is right because only attitude covers the contrast between the two persons. Exam trap: pessimism and hope are tempting because each fits one of the two people, but the item asks what the pair together shows, the difference in outlook. Key note: pessimism describes only the person looking at the thorns, and hope only the one looking at the sky; the item contrasts the two outlooks, which is attitude, so b stands.", "traps": {"a": "Prejudice is a negative attitude toward people because of their group membership; the stem has no social group, only two outlooks on the same view.", "c": "Pessimism (expecting bad outcomes) fits only the person gazing at the thorns; it cannot describe the person looking at the sky, so it misses the contrast the stem builds.", "d": "Hope fits only the person looking at the sky; it says nothing about the one fixed on the thorns, so it misses the contrast between the two."}}, {"q": "Aggression can be:", "concept": "The answer is d: aggression can be morally justifiable sometimes, is usually morally unjustifiable, and is seen in action in high-pressure games, so all of these is correct. Social psychologists define aggression as behaviour aimed at harming another living being who wants to avoid that harm (Robert Baron and Deborah Richardson, Human Aggression, 1994). The definition is about intent and behaviour, not about whether the act is good or bad, which is why all three options hold. First, some aggression is sanctioned or prosocial: force used in self-defence, by a police officer stopping an attacker, or by a parent pulling a child away from a dangerous dog. Society and most moral codes accept such acts. Second, most aggression is morally unjustifiable: bullying, domestic violence, riots and assault harm people for no defensible reason. Third, competitive sport shows aggression in action. Psychologists split aggression by its goal: in hostile aggression harm is the end in itself, hot and impulsive; in instrumental aggression harm is a means to another end, cold and planned. A hard foul to stop a certain goal in a tense final is instrumental; a punch thrown after the whistle is hostile. Example: police constable Kabir pinning down a knife attacker is justified; a senior student hitting Arjun for his lunch money is not; and Riya's deliberately hard tackle to stop a goal in a close football final is instrumental aggression under pressure. Option d is right because each statement describes a real form of aggression. Exam trap: treating aggression as always evil; the definition rests on intent to harm, so justified and unjustified acts both count.", "traps": {"a": "True, since sanctioned aggression such as self-defence or a police officer stopping an attacker can be justified; but alone it leaves out unjustified aggression and its display in sport.", "b": "True of most aggression, such as bullying and assault; but alone it denies the justified cases and ignores the aggression seen in competitive games.", "c": "True, since high-pressure sport shows both instrumental and hostile aggression; but alone it ignores the moral side covered by options a and b."}}, {"q": "\"If a Powerful country invades a poor country based on the opinion of few policy makers only\" it demonstrates concept of:", "concept": "The answer is b: a powerful country invading a poor one on the opinion of a few policy makers demonstrates groupthink. Irving Janis (Victims of Groupthink, 1972; revised 1982) defined groupthink as a mode of thinking in a highly cohesive group in which the drive for agreement overrides a realistic appraisal of the alternatives. Its conditions are a close-knit group, insulation from outside opinion, a directive leader who signals the preferred answer, no method for weighing options, and high stress. Janis listed eight symptoms: an illusion of invulnerability, belief in the group's own morality, collective rationalisation of warnings, stereotyped views of the enemy, self-censorship by doubters, an illusion of unanimity, direct pressure on dissenters, and self-appointed mindguards who keep bad news away. His classic case was the 1961 Bay of Pigs invasion of Cuba, planned by John F. Kennedy's small circle of advisers: experienced, intelligent people who approved a badly flawed plan because nobody wanted to break the consensus. The stem has the same shape: a decision with huge consequences, taken by a few insiders without real debate or outside checks. Janis's remedies follow from the causes: the leader should hold back an opinion, a member should play devil's advocate, and outside experts should be invited to challenge the plan. Option b is right because the scene is a small, insulated decision group reaching a costly consensus. Exam trap: group polarisation (the risky shift, James Stoner, 1961) also concerns group decisions, but it means discussion pushing a group towards a more extreme position, not the suppression of dissent; and the words \"powerful\" and \"poor\" pull students towards political or economic labels that are not group processes.", "traps": {"a": "Communalism is loyalty to one's own religious or ethnic community set against others, a term common in Indian politics; it describes group identity and conflict, not how a small team reaches a decision.", "c": "Crony capitalism is business success won through close ties with politicians (licences, contracts, favours); it is an economic arrangement, not a flawed group decision process.", "d": "Wrong because option b names the process exactly: a cohesive, insulated group making a costly decision without critical appraisal."}}, {"q": "\"Choosing between working at a toxic workplace or being unemployed\" demonstrates:", "concept": "The answer is b: choosing between working at a toxic workplace and being unemployed is an avoidance-avoidance conflict. Kurt Lewin (1935) described motivational conflicts in terms of valences: a goal with positive valence attracts us (approach) and one with negative valence repels us (avoidance). Crossing the two gives three basic types. In approach-approach conflict a person must choose between two attractive goals, such as two good job offers; it is the easiest to resolve, because either choice is a gain. In avoidance-avoidance conflict a person must choose between two unwanted options, the \"between the devil and the deep sea\" situation. In approach-avoidance conflict a single goal both attracts and repels. Neal Miller (1944) explained the typical behaviour with gradients: the tendency to avoid something grows stronger as one gets closer to it. So in an avoidance-avoidance conflict, moving towards one evil makes it feel worse, the person swings back towards the other, and the result is vacillation, delay and stress; people often try to leave the field altogether, for example by looking for a third option or putting off the decision. In the stem the toxic workplace is unwanted, and unemployment is unwanted too; the person must accept one of them. Option b is right because both alternatives carry negative valence and one must be chosen. Exam trap: the job brings a salary, so students call it approach-avoidance; but that type needs one goal with good and bad sides, while here two separate options are framed as evils. When each of several options has both pros and cons, the type is double approach-avoidance.", "traps": {"a": "Approach-approach conflict is a choice between two attractive goals (two good job offers); neither a toxic job nor unemployment is attractive.", "c": "Frustration is the blocking of a goal by an obstacle or a personal limit; the stem describes being forced to choose between two options, which is conflict, not frustration.", "d": "Approach-avoidance conflict involves one goal with both attractive and repellent features (a well-paid job in a city one dislikes); here there are two separate options, both framed as unwanted."}}, {"q": "In context of communication complete the series Kilo, Lima, Mike, _______.", "concept": "The answer is a: Kilo, Lima, Mike is followed by November. These are code words from the NATO phonetic alphabet, properly the radiotelephony spelling alphabet of the International Civil Aviation Organization (ICAO), used in aviation, the armed forces, shipping and radio communication. Each letter has one fixed word, chosen to be understood across languages and over noisy lines: Alfa, Bravo, Charlie, Delta, Echo, Foxtrot, Golf, Hotel, India, Juliett, Kilo, Lima, Mike, November, Oscar, Papa, Quebec, Romeo, Sierra, Tango, Uniform, Victor, Whiskey, X-ray, Yankee, Zulu. It exists because letters such as B, D, P and V, or M and N, sound alike on a crackling radio. So Kilo, Lima and Mike stand for K, L and M, and the next letter, N, is November. Option a is right because November is the code word for N. Exam trap: all four options begin with N, so the first letter alone does not decide; only November belongs to the code word list.", "traps": {"b": "\"Note Book\" starts with N, but it is not a code word in the phonetic alphabet; N is always November.", "c": "\"Newspaper\" starts with N, but the radiotelephony alphabet assigns no such word; the series needs the fixed code word November.", "d": "\"New Delhi\" is a place name beginning with N; place names such as India appear in the alphabet, but the word for N is November."}}, {"q": "Which of the following genes has relevance with leadership qualities?", "concept": "The answer is b: the genetic variant rs4950 has been linked with leadership. Jan-Emmanuel De Neve, Slava Mikhaylov, Christopher Dawes, Nicholas Christakis and James Fowler (2013) reported the finding in The Leadership Quarterly in a paper titled \"Born to lead?\". They first used twin data from the US National Longitudinal Study of Adolescent Health to estimate how heritable leadership role occupancy is, that is, whether a person holds a supervisory or managerial position at work. The estimate was about 24 per cent, meaning roughly a quarter of the variation between people in that sample was linked to genes. They then tested specific markers and found that rs4950, a single-nucleotide polymorphism (a one-letter difference in the DNA code) in the CHRNB3 gene, which codes part of a nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, was associated with holding leadership roles, and the association appeared again in a second sample, the Framingham Heart Study. Numbers beginning with \"rs\" are reference SNP identifiers from the dbSNP database, so the other options look plausible but are distractors. The press called rs4950 the first \"leadership gene\", which overstates it: it is one small association, and De Neve himself said the conventional view of leadership as a skill remains largely true, since about three quarters of the variation was not genetic. The finding gives modest support to the old trait or great-person view of leadership, set against situational theories. Option b is right because rs4950 is the marker named in this research. Exam trap: reading \"gene linked to leadership\" as \"gene that makes leaders\"; the study shows a statistical association with role occupancy, not a cause.", "traps": {"a": "rs5050 is a real reference SNP, but it lies in the angiotensinogen (AGT) gene, which is involved in blood pressure regulation; it is not the marker De Neve and colleagues linked to leadership role occupancy.", "c": "rs1000 is another real dbSNP identifier in the same \"rs\" format; no leadership association was reported for it in this research.", "d": "rs7777 is also a real dbSNP identifier (in the CXCL12 chemokine gene), but the leadership-linked variant was rs4950 in the CHRNB3 gene."}}, {"q": "Which psychologist identified autocratic, democratic & laissez faire leadership types?", "concept": "The answer is a: Kurt Lewin identified the autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire leadership styles. Kurt Lewin (1890 to 1947), often called the father of modern social psychology, ran the classic study with Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939). Adults led after-school clubs of ten-year-old boys making masks and models, and each leader was trained to create one of three social climates. The autocratic leader made every decision and gave orders; the boys worked hard while he watched but stopped when he left the room, and they grew either hostile or apathetic, with more aggression and scapegoating. The democratic leader guided the group to make its own decisions; work was more original, it continued when the leader was absent, and the boys liked this style best. The laissez-faire leader left the boys to themselves; little was done and the group was the most disorganised. The study launched the experimental study of group climates and fed Lewin's work on group dynamics and action research. A common criticism is that it used small samples of American boys. Example: three seniors run Meera's college fest committee. Arjun decides everything himself, Riya talks options through and lets the team vote, and Kabir lets everyone do as they like; Riya's team, like Lewin's democratic groups, keeps working even when she is away. Option a is right because the three styles come from Lewin, Lippitt and White's study. Exam trap: Jacob Moreno (1934) also studied groups and Albert Bandura (1961) also studied children's behaviour, so each sounds possible, but neither proposed these leadership styles; later labels such as transactional and transformational leadership come from other theorists.", "traps": {"b": "Jacob Levy Moreno (1889 to 1974) founded sociometry (Who Shall Survive?, 1934), mapping who chooses whom in a group, and psychodrama; he did not classify leadership styles.", "c": "Albert Bandura is known for social learning (the Bobo doll studies, 1961) and self-efficacy (1977); he did not identify autocratic, democratic and laissez-faire leadership.", "d": "Wrong because option a, Kurt Lewin, names the psychologist behind the 1939 leadership climate study."}}, {"q": "Identify the correct statement:", "concept": "The answer is d: DSM-5 is published by the American Psychiatric Association. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders is the American handbook of psychiatric diagnosis: each disorder is defined by explicit criteria (which symptoms, how many, for how long), so that two clinicians seeing the same patient should reach the same label. DSM-I appeared in 1952; DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer, introduced the criteria format; DSM-IV followed in 1994. The fifth edition came out in May 2013 and switched from Roman to Arabic numerals so that later updates could be numbered like software versions (5.1, 5.2); in practice the first major revision was published as DSM-5-TR, the text revision of March 2022. So the correct title is DSM-5, not DSM-V. Option d is right because it pairs the correct numeral with the correct publisher. The other major system, the International Classification of Diseases (ICD), is published by the World Health Organization and is the official system in India and most of the world. Exam trap: two look-alikes are mixed at once. Both the American Psychiatric Association and the American Psychological Association call themselves APA; the psychiatrists' APA publishes the DSM, while the psychologists' APA publishes the Publication Manual (APA referencing style) and the psychologists' ethics code. And 'DSM-V' was the planning-stage name (as in the 2002 'Research Agenda for DSM-V'), but the published book is DSM-5.", "traps": {"a": "DSM-V was the planning-stage name; the published manual uses the Arabic numeral, DSM-5. The publisher is right but the title is wrong.", "b": "Wrong twice: the numeral should be 5, and the American Psychological Association does not publish the DSM; it publishes the APA Publication Manual and the psychologists' ethics code.", "c": "The numeral is right but the publisher is wrong: the American Psychological Association is the psychologists' body, while the DSM comes from the American Psychiatric Association."}}, {"q": "Which of the following depicts abnormal behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is d: excessive attention seeking, sleeping too much and SAD can all depict abnormal behaviour. No single test separates normal from abnormal, so clinicians weigh several criteria together. Statistical rarity calls abnormal whatever is far from the average; violation of social norms calls abnormal whatever breaks a society's rules; maladaptiveness and personal distress ask whether the behaviour harms the person's life or causes suffering. Ronald Comer's Abnormal Psychology textbook condenses these into the four Ds: deviance, distress, dysfunction and danger. Each option fits once it is persistent and impairing. Seeking too much attention, such as needing to be the centre of attention and feeling uncomfortable when not, is a core feature of histrionic personality disorder. Sleeping too much (hypersomnia) appears in depression, especially with atypical features, and in hypersomnolence disorder. SAD is seasonal affective disorder, depression that returns every autumn or winter and lifts in spring, described by Norman Rosenthal and colleagues in 1984; DSM-5 records it as depression 'with seasonal pattern'. The same abbreviation is also used for social anxiety disorder, which is equally a disorder, so either reading qualifies. Option d is right because each behaviour meets at least one of the four Ds when it is frequent, lasting and disabling. For example, if Kabir sleeps 13 hours a day for months and still cannot get to college, his sleep is deviant and dysfunctional, not just lazy. Exam trap: students pick the single option that sounds most clinical (SAD); in an 'All of these' item, check whether every option can meet the criteria. Key note: none of these is abnormal in a mild or passing form (everyone sleeps in after an exam or wants attention sometimes); the item treats them as clinical signs, so d stands.", "traps": {"a": "Excessive attention seeking (a core sign of histrionic personality disorder) is abnormal when persistent, but choosing it alone leaves out hypersomnia and SAD, which qualify too.", "b": "Hypersomnia, sleeping too much, is a symptom of depression and of hypersomnolence disorder, but it is only one of three valid signs listed.", "c": "SAD (seasonal affective disorder, or social anxiety disorder) is a recognised disorder, yet attention seeking and hypersomnia also depict abnormal behaviour, so the single option is incomplete."}}, {"q": "From biological perspective imbalance in _______ may lead to abnormal behaviour.", "concept": "The answer is a: from the biological perspective, an imbalance in neurotransmitters may lead to abnormal behaviour. The biological (medical) model holds that mental disorders, like physical illnesses, arise from the body: genes, brain structure and brain chemistry. Neurotransmitters are the chemical messengers that carry signals across the synapse from one neuron to the next, and too much or too little activity in a transmitter system is linked to particular disorders. The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia holds that overactive dopamine pathways produce hallucinations and delusions; it rests on the findings that antipsychotic drugs block dopamine D2 receptors and that amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can cause psychosis. The monoamine hypothesis of depression links low mood to reduced serotonin and norepinephrine activity, which is why selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are prescribed. Low GABA activity is linked to anxiety, and benzodiazepines work by boosting GABA. Modern research sees these as simplified models, but they remain the standard illustration of a biological cause. Option a is right because neurotransmitter imbalance is the textbook example of a biological cause, alongside genes and brain damage, and it guides biological treatment with drugs. For example, if Meera's psychiatrist explains her depression as a serotonin problem and prescribes fluoxetine, he is reasoning from the biological perspective. Exam trap: blood sugar and cholesterol are also bodily, so they look 'biological', but they are general medical markers, not the mechanism the biological model of psychopathology names. Low blood sugar can make someone shaky, confused or irritable, but that is a medical condition affecting behaviour, not a theory of mental disorder.", "traps": {"b": "Blood sugar is a metabolic measure; very low levels (hypoglycaemia) can cause confusion and irritability, but this is a medical condition, not the biological model's explanation of mental disorder.", "c": "Cholesterol is a blood fat linked to heart disease; it is not the brain chemistry the biological perspective uses to explain abnormal behaviour.", "d": "'None of these' fails because neurotransmitter imbalance is the classic biological cause named in abnormal psychology texts."}}, {"q": "The statement \"A person who has been subjected to trauma during childhood may experience OCD\" exemplifies:", "concept": "The answer is a: the statement exemplifies the psychodynamic perspective. The psychodynamic perspective, founded by Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), holds that adult symptoms grow out of early childhood experiences and unresolved unconscious conflicts. Painful experiences are pushed out of awareness by defence mechanisms, yet they keep influencing behaviour and can return in disguised form as symptoms. Freud traced obsessional neurosis (today's obsessive-compulsive disorder, OCD) to conflicts of the anal stage, around ages 1 to 3, over control, dirt and order; in his Rat Man case (1909) a young lawyer's obsessive fears were linked to buried hostility towards his father. On this view, obsessions and compulsions are defences, such as undoing and isolation of affect, that hold anxiety from the childhood conflict in check. Option a is right because the statement explains an adult disorder by a childhood cause, which is the defining logic of the psychodynamic model. Exam trap: the cognitive perspective also explains OCD, but through faulty thinking in the present, not through childhood. Paul Salkovskis (1985) argued that people with OCD feel inflated responsibility for preventing harm, and Stanley Rachman's (1993) thought-action fusion makes them believe that thinking a bad thought is as bad as doing it. A stem that names a thought pattern as the cause points to cognitive; a stem that names a childhood event points to psychodynamic. The social perspective looks to poverty, stigma, culture and support. Key note: a life-span developmental view also links early events to later outcomes, so c is not absurd; but it is a framework for studying change across life rather than a model of the causes of psychopathology, and the classic perspective that explains adult disorder by childhood experience is psychodynamic; a stands.", "traps": {"b": "The cognitive perspective (for OCD, Paul Salkovskis's inflated responsibility and Stanley Rachman's thought-action fusion) explains symptoms through present faulty beliefs and appraisals, not through childhood trauma.", "c": "The life-span developmental perspective (Paul Baltes, 1987) describes growth and change from birth to death; it is a way of studying development, not the classic model that blames childhood conflict for adult disorder.", "d": "The social (sociocultural) perspective explains disorder through social conditions such as poverty, discrimination, culture and lack of support, not through one person's childhood trauma."}}, {"q": "Specific Phobia in children may be expressed by:", "concept": "The answer is d: in children, specific phobia may be expressed by freezing, crying and tantrums, so all of these. A specific phobia is a marked fear or anxiety about a particular object or situation, such as dogs, heights, injections, darkness or thunder. Under DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) the object almost always provokes immediate fear; it is actively avoided or endured with intense distress; the fear is out of proportion to the actual danger; and it usually lasts 6 months or more and causes distress or impairment. Adults can usually describe their fear in words, but young children often cannot, so the fear shows in behaviour. DSM-5 therefore adds a note to criterion A: in children, the fear or anxiety may be expressed by crying, tantrums, freezing or clinging. Each option is one of these listed signs. Freezing is the still, rooted response of the fight, flight or freeze system; crying is the plain distress signal; tantrums are explosive protest when the child is pushed towards the feared object. For example, four-year-old Arjun freezes, then screams and throws himself on the floor whenever the neighbour's dog appears; he cannot say 'I am afraid of dogs', but his behaviour says it. Option d is right because DSM-5 names all three expressions (plus clinging), so any single option leaves out the others. Exam trap: DSM-IV required adults to recognise that their fear was excessive but exempted children, and DSM-5 dropped the insight requirement altogether, so a child never needs to say the fear is unreasonable. Do not confuse the child note with the bodily symptoms of a panic attack (palpitations, sweating, trembling).", "traps": {"a": "Freezing is one of the DSM-5 child expressions of phobic fear, but crying and tantrums are listed too, so choosing it alone is incomplete.", "b": "Crying is in DSM-5's note on children, but so are freezing, tantrums and clinging; a single option misses the rest.", "c": "Tantrums are a DSM-5 child expression of phobic fear, yet freezing and crying are equally listed, so this option is only part of the answer."}}, {"q": "The risk & prognostic factors of panic disorder may be ______ in nature.", "concept": "The answer is d: DSM-5 lists temperamental, environmental and genetic (genetic and physiological) risk and prognostic factors for panic disorder, so all of these. Panic disorder means recurrent, unexpected panic attacks (sudden surges of intense fear that peak within minutes, with palpitations, sweating, trembling, breathlessness, dizziness and fear of dying or losing control), followed by at least 1 month of worry about further attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour, such as avoiding exercise. For most disorders, DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) groups risk and prognostic factors under three headings, and panic disorder has entries under all of them. Temperamental: negative affectivity (neuroticism) and anxiety sensitivity, the belief that anxiety symptoms are harmful (Steven Reiss and Richard McNally, 1985). Environmental: childhood sexual and physical abuse are reported more often than in some other anxiety disorders, smoking is a risk factor, and most people describe stressors, such as interpersonal problems or illness, in the months before their first attack. Genetic and physiological: many genes each add a little vulnerability, risk is higher in children of parents with anxiety, depressive or bipolar disorders, and breathing problems such as asthma are linked to panic. For example, Meera, a lifelong worrier who smokes and has just lost her job, carries risks under all three headings. Option d is right because each heading appears in the DSM-5 text. Exam trap: students choose 'genetic' because panic runs in families, but DSM-5 lists temperament, environment and biology side by side, a diathesis-stress picture in which they combine, so no single heading is the whole story.", "traps": {"a": "Temperamental factors (negative affectivity and anxiety sensitivity) are listed for panic disorder, but so are environmental and genetic ones, so this is only one of three headings.", "b": "Environmental factors (childhood abuse, smoking, recent stressors) appear in DSM-5, yet temperamental and genetic factors are listed too, so the single option is incomplete.", "c": "Genetic and physiological factors (many genes, family history, asthma) are real, but DSM-5 also names temperamental and environmental risks, so genetic alone misses two of the three headings."}}, {"q": "Unspecified Psychosis is represented as:", "concept": "The answer is a: unspecified psychosis is coded F29. Chapter V (F) of the World Health Organization's ICD-10 (1992) gives every mental disorder a code beginning with F, grouped in blocks of up to ten. F00 to F09 are organic disorders such as dementia and delirium; F10 to F19 substance use; F20 to F29 schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders; F30 to F39 mood (affective) disorders; F40 to F48 neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders; F50 to F59 behavioural syndromes with physical factors, starting with eating disorders. The last code in a block is often the 'unspecified' rest category. F20 is schizophrenia and F29 is unspecified nonorganic psychosis, used when a person clearly has psychotic symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganised speech) but there is not enough information for a more specific diagnosis. DSM-5 uses the same code for its 'unspecified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder', because American DSM codes are taken from ICD-10-CM. For example, Kabir is brought to the emergency ward hearing voices, with no history available yet; the psychiatrist may record F29 until the picture becomes clear. Option a is right because F29 closes the psychotic block F20 to F29 as its unspecified category. Exam trap: the neighbouring codes look close. F30 is a manic episode, the first code of the mood block, only one digit away but a different family; F45 is somatoform disorders; F50 is eating disorders such as anorexia nervosa (F50.0) and bulimia nervosa (F50.2). Useful anchors: F20 schizophrenia, F31 bipolar affective disorder, F32 depressive episode, F42 obsessive-compulsive disorder.", "traps": {"b": "F30 is a manic episode, the first code of the mood (affective) disorders block F30 to F39, not psychosis in the schizophrenia block.", "c": "F45 is somatoform disorders (such as somatisation disorder and hypochondriacal disorder), in the neurotic, stress-related and somatoform block F40 to F48.", "d": "F50 is eating disorders (anorexia nervosa F50.0, bulimia nervosa F50.2), the start of the block for behavioural syndromes with physical factors."}}, {"q": "Cyclothymia is also known as:", "concept": "The answer is c: cyclothymia is informally called bipolar III disorder. Cyclothymic disorder (DSM-5) is a chronic, fluctuating mood disturbance: for at least 2 years (1 year in children and adolescents) the person has numerous periods of hypomanic symptoms and numerous periods of depressive symptoms, but none ever meets the full criteria for a hypomanic, manic or major depressive episode, the symptoms are present at least half the time, and the person is never symptom-free for more than 2 months at a time. The term cyclothymia comes from the German psychiatrists Karl Kahlbaum and Ewald Hecker in the late nineteenth century. Its place in the bipolar family explains the nickname. Bipolar I disorder needs at least one full manic episode; bipolar II disorder needs at least one hypomanic episode plus at least one major depressive episode; cyclothymia is the milder, subthreshold third form, so popular and some clinical writing calls it 'bipolar III'. ICD-10 codes it F34.0, a persistent mood disorder, beside dysthymia (F34.1). For example, Riya has had 3 years of short 'high' spells, when she sleeps little and starts many projects, alternating with flat, low weeks, but never a full episode; this is cyclothymia. Option c is right because cyclothymia is neither bipolar I nor bipolar II, and 'bipolar III' is the name used for it among these choices. Exam trap: students pick bipolar II because both involve hypomanic swings, but bipolar II requires a full major depressive episode, which cyclothymia never reaches. Key note: DSM-5 and ICD never use 'bipolar III', and its meaning varies between bipolar spectrum schemes (in Hagop Akiskal and Olavo Pinto's 1999 scheme it means hypomania brought on by antidepressants), so the alias is informal; but it is the label the item wants, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Bipolar I disorder requires at least one full manic episode; cyclothymia by definition never reaches full mania.", "b": "Bipolar II disorder requires a full hypomanic episode and a full major depressive episode; cyclothymic swings stay below both thresholds.", "d": "'None of these' fails because cyclothymia does have a popular alias among the options: bipolar III, the milder third member of the bipolar family."}}, {"q": "Which psychological test is used to assess the symptoms of schizophrenia?", "concept": "The answer is d: SAPS, CAINS and SANS are all used to assess the symptoms of schizophrenia. Schizophrenia symptoms fall into two broad groups. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience: hallucinations, delusions, bizarre behaviour and formal thought disorder. Negative symptoms are losses: flat affect, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of drive), anhedonia and asociality. Rating scales turn a clinical interview into scores, so severity can be tracked over time or compared in research. Nancy Andreasen built two companion scales at the University of Iowa: the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS, 1982 to 1983) and the Scale for the Assessment of Positive Symptoms (SAPS, 1984). The Clinical Assessment Interview for Negative Symptoms (CAINS; Ann Kring, Raquel Gur, Jack Blanchard, William Horan and Steven Reise, 2013) is a newer interview that grew out of a US National Institute of Mental Health consensus that negative symptoms needed better measurement; it rates motivation and pleasure separately from expression. Option d is right because each scale assesses a set of schizophrenia symptoms: SAPS the positive ones, SANS and CAINS the negative ones. For example, a psychologist assessing Arjun might use SAPS to rate his voices and suspiciousness, and SANS or CAINS to rate his flat face and loss of drive. Exam trap: students may think only positive symptoms define schizophrenia, or may not recognise the newer CAINS; all three are schizophrenia instruments. Other well-known ones are the Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS; Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein and Lewis Opler, 1987) and the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS; John Overall and Donald Gorham, 1962).", "traps": {"a": "SAPS (Nancy Andreasen, 1984) rates positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions, but it is only one of three schizophrenia scales listed.", "b": "CAINS (Ann Kring and colleagues, 2013) is a negative symptom interview for schizophrenia, but SAPS and SANS assess schizophrenia too, so it is incomplete alone.", "c": "SANS (Nancy Andreasen, 1982 to 1983) rates negative symptoms such as alogia and flat affect, yet SAPS and CAINS are equally schizophrenia instruments."}}, {"q": "The drug sertraline hydrochloride is used mostly for treatment of:", "concept": "The answer is a: sertraline is used mostly for treating depression. Sertraline hydrochloride (brand name Zoloft, from Pfizer) is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor (SSRI). After a neuron releases serotonin into the synapse, a transporter protein (the serotonin transporter, SERT) normally pumps it back in. Sertraline blocks this transporter, so serotonin stays longer in the synapse and keeps acting on the receiving neuron. The mood benefit takes several weeks to appear, even though the blocking happens within hours, which suggests that slower changes in receptors and brain plasticity do the real work. It was approved in the United States in 1991 for major depressive disorder, its first and main indication; later approvals added obsessive-compulsive disorder, panic disorder, post-traumatic stress disorder, social anxiety disorder and premenstrual dysphoric disorder. SSRIs became first-line antidepressants because they are far safer in overdose than the older tricyclics (imipramine) and do not need the diet rules of monoamine oxidase inhibitors. For example, when Meera's psychiatrist diagnoses moderate depression, an SSRI such as sertraline or fluoxetine is a common first prescription. Option a is right because depression is the primary use of sertraline and of the antidepressant class it belongs to. Exam trap: SSRIs are also widely used for anxiety disorders and PTSD, so b and c tempt; the word 'mostly' asks for the main indication. Key note: anxiety disorders and PTSD (trauma) are real, approved uses of sertraline, so b and c are not false uses; but 'mostly' points to its primary indication, depression, and a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Anxiety-related conditions (panic disorder, social anxiety disorder, OCD) are approved later uses of sertraline, but they are secondary; its first and main indication is depression.", "c": "PTSD is an approved use (sertraline and paroxetine are the only two drugs approved for it in the United States), but trauma is not what the drug is mostly prescribed for.", "d": "'None of these' fails because sertraline is a standard first-line antidepressant."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an alternative for Electro convulsive therapy?", "concept": "The answer is a: transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS) is an alternative to electroconvulsive therapy. Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT), introduced by Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini in Rome in 1938, passes a brief electric current through the brain to produce a controlled seizure under general anaesthesia and a muscle relaxant. It is the most effective treatment for severe, treatment-resistant or psychotic depression, but it needs anaesthesia and can cause temporary confusion and memory loss. TMS, introduced by Anthony Barker and colleagues in Sheffield in 1985, places a magnetic coil against the scalp; rapidly changing magnetic pulses pass through the skull and induce small electric currents in the cortex underneath. Repetitive TMS (rTMS) over the left dorsolateral prefrontal cortex was cleared by the US Food and Drug Administration in 2008 for depression that had not responded to medication. The patient stays awake, no seizure is induced, no anaesthesia is needed and memory is not affected, so people can return to work the same day. For example, Kabir, whose depression has not lifted after two antidepressants and who fears the memory effects of ECT, may be offered daily rTMS sessions over several weeks. Option a is right because TMS is a brain stimulation treatment for the same main condition, depression, without ECT's seizure and anaesthesia. Exam trap: TMS is an alternative, not an equal; for the most severe or psychotic depression ECT remains more effective. CNS and PNS sound like treatment acronyms, but they are the central and peripheral nervous systems, the parts of the body that treatments act on.", "traps": {"b": "CNS is the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord), an anatomical division, not a treatment.", "c": "PNS is the peripheral nervous system (the somatic and autonomic nerves outside the brain and spinal cord), an anatomical division, not a treatment.", "d": "'All of these' fails because only TMS is a therapy; CNS and PNS are parts of the nervous system."}}, {"q": "CBT can be used for:", "concept": "The answer is d: CBT can be used for lifestyle management, emotional problems and behavioural problems, so all of these. Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) joins two traditions. Behaviour therapy, built on conditioning research (Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, 1958), changes what people do. Cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron Beck in the 1960s, with Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (1955) as a forerunner, changes how people interpret events. The core model links thoughts, feelings and behaviour: change one and the others shift. Therapy is structured, time-limited and collaborative, with homework, thought records and behavioural experiments. Each option is a recognised use. Emotional problems: CBT is a first-line treatment for depression and the anxiety disorders, including panic, social anxiety and PTSD. Behavioural problems: it treats anger, conduct problems in children (through parent training), substance misuse and habits such as hair pulling. Lifestyle management: CBT for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia, and CBT methods help with weight control, quitting smoking, stress and sticking to treatment in diabetes or heart disease. For example, Arjun uses one CBT programme to challenge the thought 'I will fail' (emotional), stop skipping classes (behavioural) and fix his sleep schedule (lifestyle). Option d is right because CBT's thought, feeling and behaviour model applies to all three areas. Exam trap: students sometimes think CBT is only for mental illness and so drop lifestyle management; health psychology uses CBT widely for health behaviour change, so each single option is incomplete.", "traps": {"a": "Lifestyle management (insomnia, weight, smoking, chronic illness) is a real CBT use, but CBT also treats emotional and behavioural problems.", "b": "Emotional problems such as depression and anxiety are CBT's best-known target, but not its only one; behaviour and lifestyle are covered too.", "c": "Behavioural problems (anger, addictions, conduct problems) are treated with CBT, but so are emotional problems and lifestyle issues, so this is incomplete."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is correct?", "concept": "The answer is a: to check prediction coefficients, both variables should preferably be measured on the same kind of scale. Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) sorted measurement into four levels. Nominal scales only name categories (blood group, religion). Ordinal scales rank (class rank, agreement ratings). Interval scales have equal units but no true zero (temperature in Celsius, IQ). Ratio scales have equal units and a true zero (reaction time, weight, income). Each level permits different statistics. A prediction (correlation or regression) coefficient summarises how well one variable predicts another, and the right coefficient depends on the scale of both variables: Pearson's r assumes both are interval or ratio; Spearman's rho both ranks; phi both true dichotomies; tetrachoric r both artificial dichotomies. So textbooks give the rule of thumb that the two variables should be measured with the same scaling technique. For example, if Riya correlates students' entrance marks with their final marks, both interval, Pearson's r fits; if one teacher only ranks the students, both variables should be ranked and Spearman's rho used. Option a is right because matching the scales lets you use the standard coefficient for that level. Statement c is false because IQ is an interval scale: a score of 0 does not mean zero intelligence, and an IQ of 140 is not twice an IQ of 70. Statement d is false because ratio scales are quantitative, so they are least, not most, common in qualitative enquiry, which works with words and themes. Exam trap: b simply reverses a, a common examiner trick. Key note: mixed-scale coefficients do exist (point-biserial r for one dichotomous and one continuous variable, biserial r, eta), so a is a rule of thumb, not a law; it is the only defensible statement here, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "This reverses the rule: mixing scales forces special mixed-scale coefficients and loses information, so textbooks prefer the same scale for both variables.", "c": "Intelligence tests give IQ scores on an interval scale with no true zero, so ratio statements such as 'twice as intelligent' are meaningless.", "d": "Ratio scales (true zero, equal units) belong to quantitative measurement; qualitative enquiry uses words and themes, at most nominal categories."}}, {"q": "SD is to normal probability curve as:", "concept": "The answer is a: SD is to the normal probability curve as sensitivity and specificity are to the ROC curve. The relation in the stem pair is 'the measure a curve is built from'. The normal curve is a symmetrical, bell-shaped distribution fully defined by two numbers, its mean and its standard deviation (SD). The SD is the curve's unit of distance: about 68% of cases lie within 1 SD of the mean, about 95% within 2 SD and about 99.7% within 3 SD. Change the SD and the curve becomes taller and narrower or flatter and wider. The receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve comes from signal detection theory, first used for radar in the Second World War. A diagnostic test is tried at every possible cut-off score; at each one we compute sensitivity (the true positive rate, the share of real cases the test catches) and specificity (the true negative rate, the share of healthy people it clears). The ROC curve plots sensitivity against 1 minus specificity, and the area under the curve (AUC) summarises accuracy: 0.5 is chance, 1.0 is perfect. For example, when Arjun evaluates a depression screening questionnaire, each cut-off gives one pair of sensitivity and specificity values, and joining the points draws his ROC curve. Option a is right because in both pairs the first term is the quantity the curve is constructed from. Exam trap: b, c and d pair two related statistics with each other (mean and mode, median and mean, correlation and regression), so they feel statistical but lack the measure-to-curve relation. Key note: analogy items are loose, and the SD defines rather than plots the normal curve; but a is the only pair where the first term is what a curve is constructed from, so a stands.", "traps": {"b": "Mean and mode are two measures of central tendency compared with each other; neither is a curve, so the measure-to-curve relation is missing.", "c": "Median and mean are both measures of central tendency, not a measure and the curve built from it.", "d": "Correlation and regression are related techniques (regression uses correlation to predict), but this pairs two methods, not a measure with its curve."}}, {"q": "The most famous case study in cognitive psychology is that of:", "concept": "The answer is c: among the options, the most famous case study is that of Phineas Gage. A case study is an in-depth study of one person, used when an event is too rare or too unethical to create on purpose. In 1848 Phineas Gage, a 25-year-old railway construction foreman in Vermont, was packing blasting powder when an explosion drove his tamping iron, over a metre long, up through his left cheek and out of the top of his skull, destroying much of his left frontal lobe. He survived, could walk and talk, and his memory and intelligence seemed intact. But his personality changed: the reliable, well-liked foreman became impulsive, profane, stubborn and unable to keep to plans. His physician John Martyn Harlow (reports of 1848 and 1868) wrote that friends found him 'no longer Gage'. In 1994 Hanna Damasio and colleagues reconstructed the injury from his skull and placed it in the ventromedial prefrontal cortex. The case became the classic evidence that the frontal lobes govern planning, emotional control, personality and social behaviour. Option c is right because Gage is the only option that is a case study of one person's mind and brain; the other options are researchers. Exam trap: John B. Watson (behaviourism, 1913), B. F. Skinner (operant conditioning, 1938) and Philip Zimbardo (prison study, 1971) are famous for experiments, not for being the subject of a case study; examiners rely on students choosing any familiar name. Key note: in cognitive psychology proper, Henry Molaison (patient H.M.; William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), whose brain surgery left him unable to form new explicit (declarative) long-term memories, is often called the most famous case, and Gage is strictly a neuropsychology case; but H.M. is not offered, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "John B. Watson founded behaviourism (1913); his Little Albert study with Rosalie Rayner (1920) was a conditioning experiment on an infant, and the option names the researcher, not a case.", "b": "B. F. Skinner is known for operant conditioning experiments with rats and pigeons in the operant chamber, not for a single-person case study.", "d": "Philip Zimbardo ran the Stanford Prison Experiment (1971), a social psychology simulation with a group of student volunteers, not a case study in cognitive psychology."}}, {"q": "The reliability of a test is expressed in terms of proportion of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the reliability of a test is the proportion true score variance divided by observed score variance. Classical test theory, built on the model Charles Spearman proposed in 1904, says every observed score (X) is a true score (T) plus random error (E): X = T + E. The true score is the average a person would earn over endless independent testings; error is whatever pushes a single score up or down by chance (fatigue, guessing, a noisy room). Because errors are random and uncorrelated with true scores, the variances add: observed variance = true variance + error variance. Reliability is the share of observed-score variance that is true-score variance: true variance / observed variance, which also equals 1 minus error variance / observed variance. It runs from 0 (all error) to 1 (no error). For example, if Meera's anxiety test produces scores with an observed variance of 100, of which 80 is true variance and 20 error, its reliability is 80 / 100 = .80: 80% of the differences between people reflect real differences and 20% are noise. Option a is right because a proportion must put the part (true variance) over the whole (observed variance). Exam trap: b turns the fraction upside down; observed over true can never be below 1 (100 / 80 = 1.25), so it cannot be a proportion. Options c and d multiply variances, and a product is not a share of anything. Test-retest, parallel forms, split-half and Cronbach's alpha are all ways of estimating this same ratio.", "traps": {"b": "Observed over true variance is the reciprocal of reliability; it is always 1 or more (100 / 80 = 1.25), so it cannot be a proportion.", "c": "Multiplying true and observed variance gives a product, not a proportion; it grows with the size of the variances and has no 0 to 1 meaning.", "d": "True variance times error variance is also a product, not a proportion; error variance enters reliability only as 1 minus error variance / observed variance."}}, {"q": "A child using proper grammar while expressing anything is likely to be in:", "concept": "The answer is c: a child who uses proper grammar in whatever they express is likely to be in the concrete operational stage. Jean Piaget (The Language and Thought of the Child, 1923) described four stages of cognitive development. Sensorimotor (birth to 2): infants know the world through actions; speech moves from babbling to first words around 12 months. Preoperational (2 to 7): the symbolic function appears and vocabulary explodes, but thinking is egocentric and intuitive. Speech shows it: egocentric speech, collective monologues (children 'talking together' without responding to each other) and many grammatical errors, especially overregularisation, applying a rule too widely ('I goed', 'two foots', 'mouses'). Concrete operational (7 to 11): thought becomes logical, decentred and reversible for concrete things; speech becomes socialised, adapted to the listener, and grammar is largely correct and stable. Formal operational (11 onward): abstract, hypothetical reasoning. Complex structures do keep maturing in the school years: Carol Chomsky (1969) showed with a blindfolded doll ('Is the doll easy to see?') that such sentences are still being mastered between ages 5 and 10. For example, five-year-old Kabir says 'I runned fast and catched it'; at nine he says 'I ran fast and caught it' and shapes his story so his listener can follow. Option c is right because consistently correct grammar across all kinds of talk is the school-age, concrete operational picture. Exam trap: language grows fastest in the preoperational stage, so students pick b; but fast growth comes with errors. Key note: much basic grammar is in place by about age 5 to 6, late in the preoperational stage, so b is arguable; but consistently proper grammar in whatever the child says is the concrete operational picture, so c stands.", "traps": {"a": "In the sensorimotor stage (0 to 2) the child moves only from babbling to first words and two-word phrases; there is no full grammar yet.", "b": "In the preoperational stage (2 to 7) language explodes, but speech is egocentric and full of errors such as overregularisation ('goed', 'foots'), so grammar is not yet consistently proper.", "d": "The formal operational stage (11 onward) adds abstract, hypothetical reasoning; proper grammar is already in place before it, so it is not the stage that marks it."}}, {"q": "A person after retirement from services falls in which of the following conflicts?", "concept": "The answer is c: a person who has retired from service faces Erik Erikson's crisis of integrity versus despair. Erik Erikson (Childhood and Society, 1950) described eight psychosocial stages from infancy to old age. Each stage poses a crisis, a turning point between two opposite outcomes, and resolving it well yields a virtue. The eighth and last stage, late adulthood (about 60 to 65 onward in most textbooks), is ego integrity versus despair. The person now looks back over the life they have lived. Integrity is accepting that life, failures included, as meaningful and one's own; despair is regret and bitterness that there is no time left to start again, often with fear of death. The virtue is wisdom. Retirement is a classic marker of entry into this stage, because the working role ends and life review begins. For example, after retiring as a bank manager, Rakesh spends his evenings recalling his career and family; if he feels 'I did my best and it mattered', that is integrity; if he feels 'I wasted my years', that is despair. Option c is right because the stem places the person at retirement, which is late adulthood, and that stage's crisis is integrity versus despair. Exam trap: the word 'conflicts' pulls students towards Kurt Lewin's (1935) choice conflicts, approach-avoidance and avoidance-avoidance; Erikson's stages are conflicts too, but between two outcomes of a life stage, and only one option belongs to the life stage of retirement. Key note: retirement can also feel like an approach-avoidance conflict (free time versus lost role and income), so a is not absurd; but the item wants the life-stage crisis, and c stands.", "traps": {"a": "Approach-avoidance is Kurt Lewin's conflict over one goal with both attractive and unattractive sides; retirement may feel like this, but it is a choice conflict, not the life-stage crisis of late adulthood.", "b": "Avoidance-avoidance is Lewin's conflict between two unwanted options (the devil and the deep sea); nothing in retirement itself forces a choice between two negatives.", "d": "Identity versus role confusion is Erikson's fifth stage, adolescence, when the young person works out who they are; it does not fit a retired adult."}}, {"q": "Maintaining Social order is a concept central to which of the following theories?", "concept": "The answer is b: maintaining social order is central to Lawrence Kohlberg's theory of moral development. Lawrence Kohlberg (doctoral dissertation, University of Chicago, 1958) presented boys with moral dilemmas, the best known being Heinz, who steals a drug he cannot afford to save his dying wife. What mattered was not whether a person said Heinz should steal, but the reasoning behind the answer. He found three levels with two stages each. Preconventional: stage 1 obedience and punishment, stage 2 self-interest and exchange. Conventional: stage 3 good boy or good girl (interpersonal approval), and stage 4 the law and order, or social-order-maintaining, orientation. Postconventional: stage 5 social contract and individual rights, stage 6 universal ethical principles. At stage 4, right means doing one's duty, respecting authority and keeping laws, because society would fall apart if everyone broke them. For example, Riya says Heinz must not steal because 'if everyone stole whenever they felt justified, society would collapse'; this is stage 4 reasoning. Option b is right because maintaining the social order names a stage in Kohlberg's sequence, the heart of his conventional level. Exam trap: the words 'social' and 'order' pull towards social identity theory, but Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979) explain how group membership shapes self-esteem and in-group bias, not moral reasoning about laws. Do not confuse stage 4 law and order with stage 5, which sees laws as social contracts that can be changed when they fail to protect rights.", "traps": {"a": "Social identity theory (Henri Tajfel and John Turner, 1979) explains how belonging to groups shapes self-concept and in-group favouritism; it is not about maintaining social order through moral reasoning.", "c": "John B. Watson's behaviourism (1913) explains behaviour through conditioning; it has no stage concerned with keeping the social order.", "d": "Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory describes eight life-stage crises (trust, autonomy, identity, integrity and so on); social order is not its central concept."}}, {"q": "\"A teacher guides a student to have mastery over skills which the student otherwise can't achieve easily\". This statement signifies:", "concept": "The answer is a: the statement describes the zone of proximal development. Lev Vygotsky (writings of the late 1920s and early 1930s; Mind in Society, English edition 1978) held that cognitive development is social: children first do things with other people and only later alone. He defined the zone of proximal development (ZPD) as the distance between what a learner can do independently (the actual developmental level) and what the learner can achieve with guidance from a more knowledgeable other, such as a teacher, parent or skilled peer (the potential level). Teaching works best inside this zone: tasks the learner can already do alone teach little, and tasks far beyond reach cannot be done even with help. 'Proximal' means nearest: the skills that are just about to develop. The support itself was later called scaffolding by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976): the teacher breaks the task into steps, gives hints, models and encourages, and withdraws help as competence grows, like scaffolding taken down once a building can stand. In the stem, the teacher guides the student to master skills the student could not easily reach alone, which is exactly teaching within the ZPD. Option a is right because it is Vygotsky's term for the gap that guidance bridges. Exam trap: the other three options are invented labels built on the same 'Zone of ...' pattern to test whether you know the real term; 'zone of personal development' sounds especially plausible but is not a psychological concept. Do not confuse scaffolding (the help) with the ZPD (the range in which help works), and remember that Jean Piaget (The Origins of Intelligence in Children, 1936), unlike Vygotsky, stressed the child's independent discovery.", "traps": {"b": "'Zone of difficulty' is not a term in developmental psychology; tasks that are simply too hard lie beyond the ZPD in Vygotsky's account.", "c": "'Zone of skill development' is an invented label that mimics the real term; Vygotsky's concept is the zone of proximal development.", "d": "'Zone of personal development' is not a recognised concept; it borrows the 'zone' wording but drops 'proximal', the idea of the next skills that guidance can unlock."}}, {"q": "Raw Scores are to Z scores as:", "concept": "The answer is a: raw scores are to z scores as covariance is to correlation, because in both pairs the second term is the first one standardised, stripped of its original units. A z score says how far a raw score lies from the mean in standard deviation units: z = (X minus mean) / SD. Every set of z scores has a mean of 0 and an SD of 1, so a raw mark of 60 becomes comparable across tests. Covariance measures how two variables vary together: the average product of each person's deviations from the two means. Its size depends on the units (it changes when centimetres become inches) and it has no fixed upper limit. Karl Pearson (1896) solved this by dividing covariance by the product of the two standard deviations: r = cov(X, Y) / (SD of X times SD of Y). The result always lies between minus 1 and plus 1, whatever the units. Put another way, r is the mean product of paired z scores: covariance computed on z scores. Example: Kabir finds a covariance of 45 between hours of study and marks, which shows direction but not strength; dividing by the two SDs gives r = 0.60, a moderate positive correlation. Option a is right because dividing by the SD turns a raw score into z, and dividing by the two SDs turns covariance into r. Exam trap: option b uses the same two terms in reverse order; in an analogy direction matters, and correlation to covariance runs from the standardised form back to the raw one. Key note: covariance to correlation is the cleaner standardisation, because r is always covariance in z-score units. Option d has a real case, since a raw regression slope standardised in both variables equals r, but only in simple regression with one predictor; with several predictors the standardised weights no longer equal r, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "This is option a's pair turned round. Going from correlation back to covariance means multiplying r by the two SDs, which puts the units back in: standardised to raw, the opposite direction of raw to z.", "c": "Correlation and regression are partner techniques: correlation measures the strength of a linear relation, and regression (from Francis Galton's work on heredity, 1880s) uses it to predict one variable from another. Regression is not correlation with the units removed, so the pair does not mirror raw to z.", "d": "In simple regression the standardised slope (beta) does equal r, but 'regression' names a whole prediction method, not a raw quantity in score units. The direct textbook case of a raw measure becoming a standardised one is covariance becoming correlation."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a goodness of fit test?", "concept": "The answer is a: the chi-square test is the classic goodness of fit test. A goodness of fit test asks how well observed data match the distribution that a hypothesis predicts. Karl Pearson (1900) introduced chi-square for exactly this purpose. You sort cases into categories, count the observed frequency (O) in each, work out the expected frequency (E) if the hypothesis were true, and add up (O minus E) squared divided by E across the categories. A small chi-square means the data fit the expected pattern; a large one, read against the chi-square table with (number of categories minus 1) degrees of freedom, means they do not. Because it works on counts in categories, it needs no normal distribution and is classed as non-parametric. Example: Meera asks 120 students which of four counselling slots they prefer. If there is no preference, each slot should get 30. She observes 50, 30, 25 and 15, and chi-square tells her whether that spread departs from the even split more than chance allows. Applied to a two-way table (are gender and choice of stream related?), the same statistic becomes the chi-square test of independence, a different use. Option a is right because chi-square compares observed counts with the counts a model expects, which is what fit means. Exam trap: the runs test is also non-parametric and also works on counts, so it looks similar, but it tests whether a sequence is random, not whether data fit a distribution. The Kolmogorov-Smirnov one-sample test is the other well-known goodness of fit test, but it is not offered here.", "traps": {"b": "The t-test of William Sealy Gosset, who published as 'Student' (1908), compares two means, or one mean with a known value, on interval data. It asks whether means differ, not whether frequencies fit a predicted distribution.", "c": "Analysis of variance, developed by Ronald Fisher in the 1920s, compares three or more group means by splitting variance into between-group and within-group parts. It tests mean differences, not goodness of fit.", "d": "The runs test (Abraham Wald and Jacob Wolfowitz, 1940) counts runs, unbroken streaks of the same symbol, to judge whether a sequence is random or whether two samples come from one population. It is a test of randomness, not of fit to an expected distribution."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a correct statement?", "concept": "The answer is b: the mean can sometimes mislead a researcher, because it uses the value of every score and so is pulled by extreme scores and by skew. The arithmetic mean is the sum of the scores divided by their number. Using every score makes it stable from sample to sample and the basis of most parametric statistics, but it also lets one outlier drag it away from where most scores lie. The scores 4, 7, 7, 9 and 13 have a mean of 8. Add a single 40 and the mean jumps to 13.3, higher than five of the six scores, while the median moves only from 7 to 8. In skewed data such as income the mean runs into the long tail: if a businessman earning crores joins a room of ten clerks, the 'average income' in the room becomes huge, though no clerk is any richer. That is why median income, not mean income, is usually reported. Example: Arjun reports that patients at his clinic waited 'on average 40 minutes', but most waited 10 to 15 minutes and two waited four hours. The median gives the truer picture. Option b is right because the mean describes the centre well only when the data are roughly symmetrical and free of outliers. Exam trap: option a sounds like textbook praise, since the mean is the most stable and most widely used average, but no measure is the most accurate in every case. The right choice depends on the scale of measurement and the shape of the distribution.", "traps": {"a": "The mean is the most stable and most widely used average, but no measure of central tendency is the most accurate for all data: for skewed data or open-ended classes the median is better, and for nominal data only the mode works.", "c": "The first half is true: the mean is the centre of gravity (balance point) of a distribution, since deviations around it sum to zero. But the median is the middle score that splits the cases into two equal halves, not the mode, so the second half of the analogy fails.", "d": "Karl Pearson's empirical rule for moderately skewed distributions is Mode = 3 Median minus 2 Mean, which gives Mean = (3 Median minus Mode) / 2. 'Mean = 3/2 Median' drops the mode term and is not a valid relation."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a parametric test?", "concept": "The answer is c: the Kruskal-Wallis test is not parametric; it is a non-parametric, rank-based test. Parametric tests estimate population parameters such as the mean, and assume interval or ratio scores that are roughly normally distributed in the population, with similar variances across groups. Non-parametric (distribution-free) tests drop the normality assumption; most turn the scores into ranks and compare the ranks. William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952) devised the H test as a one-way ANOVA by ranks. All scores from three or more independent groups are pooled and ranked, and H checks whether the average rank differs between the groups, read against chi-square with k minus 1 degrees of freedom. Each common parametric test has a rank partner: unpaired t-test and Mann-Whitney U (two independent groups); paired t-test and Wilcoxon signed-rank (two related samples); one-way ANOVA and Kruskal-Wallis (three or more independent groups); repeated-measures ANOVA and Friedman (three or more related samples). Example: Kabir compares pain ratings on a 1 to 10 scale across three hospital wards. The ratings are ordinal and badly skewed, so he uses Kruskal-Wallis instead of ANOVA. Option c is right because Kruskal-Wallis works on ranks and makes no assumption of a normal population. Exam trap: the paired t-test is often mistaken for a non-parametric test because it handles related samples, and ANOVA because it compares several groups; both still assume normal, interval data. The non-parametric partner of the paired t-test is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test.", "traps": {"a": "The unpaired (independent-samples) t-test, built on the t distribution of William Sealy Gosset ('Student', 1908), compares two independent group means and assumes interval data, normality and equal variances, so it is parametric. Its rank-based partner is the Mann-Whitney U test.", "b": "Ronald Fisher's analysis of variance (1920s) compares three or more means and assumes normality and homogeneity of variance, so it is parametric. Kruskal-Wallis is in fact its non-parametric alternative.", "d": "The paired (related-samples) t-test compares two measurements on the same people and assumes the differences are normally distributed, so it is parametric. Its non-parametric partner is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test."}}, {"q": "If a child is able to ask you about your wellbeing he is likely to have achieved _______ to some extent.", "concept": "The answer is c: a child who asks how you are has reached some socio-emotional development, because the act joins an emotional skill with a social one. Socio-emotional (psychosocial) development is the domain of change in emotions, self-understanding and relationships, beside the physical and cognitive domains. To ask 'Are you feeling better?' a child must first read or imagine another person's feelings, an emotional capacity built on empathy. Martin Hoffman (Empathy and Moral Development, 2000) traced empathy from global empathy in infancy (a baby cries when another cries) to concern for another's feelings from about age 2 to 3. The child also needs a theory of mind, the understanding that others have inner states that can differ from one's own. David Premack and Guy Woodruff (1978) coined the term, and Heinz Wimmer and Josef Perner (1983) devised the false-belief task, which most children pass from about age 4 to 5. Finally the child must turn that understanding into a caring social act: starting a conversation, showing concern, waiting for the answer. Example: four-year-old Anaya sees her grandmother lying down and asks, 'Nani, does your head hurt?' Option c is right because the behaviour needs both emotional understanding and social skill, and socio-emotional development is the term that covers the two together. Exam trap: social development and emotional development each describe only half of the act, and socio-cultural development sounds close but refers to Lev Vygotsky's theory (Mind in Society, English edition 1978) that culture and social interaction shape thinking.", "traps": {"a": "Social development covers relationships and social skills such as making friends, sharing and taking turns. Asking about someone's wellbeing is social, but it also rests on empathy and understanding of feelings, so 'social' alone covers only half.", "b": "Emotional development covers recognising, expressing and regulating feelings. Sensing that someone is unwell is emotional, but turning it into a caring question is a social act, so 'emotional' alone is incomplete.", "d": "Socio-cultural development comes from Lev Vygotsky's theory that culture, language and guided interaction (the zone of proximal development) drive cognitive growth. It is about how thinking develops, not about empathy and caring behaviour."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not usually covered in a health belief model?", "concept": "The answer is d: perceived social support is not part of the health belief model. The health belief model (HBM) was built in the 1950s by social psychologists in the US Public Health Service, Godfrey Hochbaum, Irwin Rosenstock and Stephen Kegeles, to explain a puzzle: why so few people came for free tuberculosis chest X-rays. Rosenstock (1966) gave it its classic statement. It says a person takes a health action when four beliefs line up: perceived susceptibility (I could get this), perceived severity (it would be serious), perceived benefits (the action would help) and perceived barriers (cost, pain, time, fear), which must be outweighed by the benefits. A cue to action, such as a symptom, a reminder or a friend's illness, triggers the step, and in 1988 Rosenstock, Victor Strecher and Marshall Becker added self-efficacy, the belief that one can carry the action out. Example: Rohan, a 45-year-old smoker, believes he could get lung cancer, that it would be deadly and that a screening scan helps, but the clinic is far away. When his uncle is diagnosed (a cue to action), he books the scan. Option d is right because social support, the sense of being cared for and helped by others, belongs to other accounts of health, such as the stress-buffering hypothesis of Sheldon Cohen and Thomas Wills (1985), and is not a component of the model. Exam trap: all four options begin with 'perceived', so the odd one hides by copying the pattern. Remember the core four as susceptibility, severity, benefits and barriers.", "traps": {"a": "Perceived susceptibility, a person's belief about how likely they are to get the illness, is one of the four core components of the health belief model.", "b": "Perceived severity, the belief about how serious the illness and its consequences would be, is a core component; with susceptibility it makes up the perceived threat.", "c": "Perceived benefits, the belief that the recommended action will reduce the threat, is a core component, weighed against the perceived barriers."}}, {"q": "Which Country tops the list of happy countries in the world as per World Happiness Report-2022?", "concept": "The answer is b: Finland topped the World Happiness Report 2022, its fifth year in a row at number one. The World Happiness Report is an annual survey-based ranking, first issued in 2012 and published for many years by the UN Sustainable Development Solutions Network; its editors have included John Helliwell, Richard Layard, Jeffrey Sachs and Jan-Emmanuel De Neve. It ranks countries mainly by life evaluations from the Gallup World Poll: people rate their lives on the Cantril ladder (Hadley Cantril, 1965) from 0, the worst possible life, to 10, the best, and the report uses a three-year average. It then explains the differences with six factors: GDP per capita, social support, healthy life expectancy, freedom to make life choices, generosity and perceived corruption. In the 2022 report Finland was first, Denmark second and Iceland third, with Norway eighth. The Nordic countries score high because of strong social support, trust in institutions, low corruption and secure welfare systems, not because of sunshine or sheer wealth. Option b is right because Finland held first place in every report from 2018 to 2022, and the 2022 edition was its fifth consecutive win. Exam trap: Norway is the classic distractor. It topped the 2017 report and is a Nordic neighbour, so a student who remembers only 'a Scandinavian country' can pick the wrong one.", "traps": {"a": "Norway topped the 2017 World Happiness Report, but in 2022 it was eighth; it is the Nordic near-miss.", "c": "France was 20th in the 2022 report, well below the Nordic countries, and has never topped the report.", "d": "The USA has never topped the report; in 2022 it was 16th."}}, {"q": "Hans Selye, the proponent of general adaptation syndrome, had core specialization in the field of:", "concept": "The answer is a: Hans Selye's core field was endocrinology, the medical science of hormones and the glands that release them. Hans Selye (1907 to 1982) was born in Vienna, trained in medicine and chemistry in Prague, and did his life's work in Canada, at McGill University and then the University of Montreal. While hunting for a new sex hormone, he injected rats with ovarian and placental extracts and found the same changes whatever he injected; cold, injury and toxic drugs produced them too. In a 1936 letter to Nature, 'A syndrome produced by diverse nocuous agents', he described the triad: enlarged adrenal cortex, shrunken thymus and lymph nodes, and stomach ulcers. He called this nonspecific response the general adaptation syndrome, with three stages, alarm, resistance and exhaustion, and popularised it in The Stress of Life (1956), later separating eustress (good stress) from distress. His evidence was hormonal throughout: the pituitary and adrenal glands carried the response. Option a is right because Selye was a physician and endocrinologist who studied glands and hormones in laboratory animals; he was never trained as a psychologist. Exam trap: because the general adaptation syndrome appears in every health psychology and biopsychology chapter, students assume Selye belonged to those fields. Health psychology only became an organised field in the late 1970s, decades after his discovery.", "traps": {"b": "Psychology was not Selye's training; he was a medical scientist. Psychologists such as Richard Lazarus (1966; with Susan Folkman, 1984) later added cognitive appraisal, which Selye's purely physiological model lacked.", "c": "Biopsychology studies how the brain, nerves and hormones relate to behaviour. It draws on Selye's work, but it is a branch of psychology, while he worked as an endocrinologist in experimental medicine.", "d": "Health psychology, the study of psychological factors in health and illness, became an organised field only in the late 1970s (APA Division 38 dates from 1978). It borrowed Selye's model; he did not belong to it."}}, {"q": "Which of the following exemplifies health enhancing behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is d: a balanced diet, wearing a seat belt and taking multivitamins are all taught as examples of health-enhancing behaviour. Stanislav Kasl and Sidney Cobb (1966) defined health behaviour as any action taken by a person who believes himself healthy, to prevent illness or detect it while there are no symptoms. They set it apart from illness behaviour (what a person does on noticing a symptom) and sick-role behaviour (what they do, once they regard themselves as ill, to get well). Health psychology texts then sort such habits into those that build fitness and wellbeing and those that guard against illness and injury. A balanced diet is the model health-enhancing habit: it supports energy and healthy weight and lowers the risk of heart disease and diabetes. Wearing a seat belt is the textbook safety habit: a small, cheap act that sharply cuts the risk of death and serious injury in a crash. Taking multivitamins is a classic preventive habit people adopt against deficiency, and for some (a pregnant woman taking folic acid, an older person short of vitamin D) it clearly helps. Example: Priya eats dal, vegetables and fruit daily, buckles up in every cab and takes the vitamin D her doctor advised. Option d is right because each act is a voluntary step taken while healthy to promote or protect health. Exam trap: students pick a alone because diet sounds like the only 'enhancing' habit; the item uses the term broadly, for any health-promoting behaviour. Key note: seat belts are more exactly called health-protective, and evidence for routine multivitamins in well-nourished adults is weak; the item groups all three as health-promoting behaviours, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "A balanced diet is a health-enhancing behaviour, but so are the other two; choosing it alone leaves out seat belts and vitamins, which the item also counts.", "b": "Wearing a seat belt is a health-protective behaviour that prevents injury. It qualifies, but on its own it leaves out diet and multivitamins.", "c": "Taking multivitamins is a preventive health habit and qualifies, but picking it alone ignores a balanced diet and seat belt use, which are even clearer examples."}}, {"q": "Who proposed the concept of positive organizational behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is a: Fred Luthans proposed the concept of positive organisational behaviour. Fred Luthans (2002), a management professor at the University of Nebraska, carried Martin Seligman's positive psychology movement (launched during Seligman's 1998 APA presidency) into the workplace. He defined positive organisational behaviour (POB) as the study and application of positively oriented human resource strengths and psychological capacities that can be measured, developed and effectively managed for better performance. His tests for including a capacity were strict: it must rest on theory and research, be measurable, be state-like (open to development through training, unlike a fixed trait) and relate to performance. The four capacities that best met these tests became psychological capital (PsyCap), remembered as HERO: hope, efficacy (confidence, from Albert Bandura's self-efficacy, 1977), resilience and optimism. Luthans, Carolyn Youssef and Bruce Avolio set this out in Psychological Capital (2007). Example: Neha, a bank branch manager in Pune, runs short workshops in which tellers set goals, plan routes around obstacles and rehearse bouncing back after an angry customer, all aimed at raising their hope, efficacy and resilience. Option a is right because both the concept and the term POB come from Luthans. Exam trap: the other three are founders of classical management and organisation theory, so all are 'organisation' names. None of them wrote about positive psychological capacities; their concern was efficiency, administration and formal structure.", "traps": {"b": "Frederick Winslow Taylor founded scientific management (The Principles of Scientific Management, 1911): time-and-motion study, one best way, pay tied to output. It treats work as a process to make efficient, not as a matter of positive psychological capacities.", "c": "Henri Fayol's administrative theory (1916) gave the five functions of management and 14 principles such as unity of command and division of work. It concerns how managers run an organisation, not positive strengths.", "d": "Max Weber (1864 to 1920) described bureaucracy, the rational-legal organisation built on rules, hierarchy and impersonal offices. It is a theory of structure and authority, not of positive organisational behaviour."}}, {"q": "As per which therapy people are responsible for their behaviour?", "concept": "The answer is a: reality therapy holds that people are responsible for their own behaviour. William Glasser (1925 to 2013), an American psychiatrist, developed it while working with delinquent girls at the Ventura School and published Reality Therapy in 1965. Its later base, choice theory (Choice Theory, 1998), says that nearly all behaviour is chosen to meet five built-in needs: survival, love and belonging, power (achievement), freedom and fun. Because behaviour is chosen, the person is responsible for it, not the past, the parents or a 'mental illness', and can learn to choose better. Glasser even spoke of 'depressing' rather than 'being depressed'. The therapist is warm but firm, focuses on present behaviour, accepts no excuses and uses no punishment. Robert Wubbolding (1980s) summed up the method as WDEP: Wants (what do you want?), Doing and direction (what are you doing?), Evaluation (is it working?) and Planning (a simple, specific plan). Example: Aditya, 16, blames his teachers for failing. The therapist asks what he wants (to pass), what he is doing (skipping classes) and whether it is working (no), then helps him plan to attend every class this week. Option a is right because personal responsibility for present, chosen behaviour is the central principle of reality therapy. Exam trap: REBT and CBT also ask clients to take ownership of their thinking, so they look plausible, but their central idea is that irrational or distorted beliefs cause distress. Key note: Albert Ellis's REBT (1955) and Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1960s) do hold people responsible for their beliefs, so b and c are not absurd; but responsibility for behaviour is the defining principle of reality therapy, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (1955) traces distress to irrational beliefs in its ABC model and disputes them. It asks clients to own their beliefs, but its defining idea is belief change, not responsibility for chosen behaviour.", "c": "Cognitive behaviour therapy, rooted in Aaron Beck's cognitive therapy (1960s), targets distorted automatic thoughts and unhelpful behaviours. Responsibility is not its organising principle.", "d": "'None of these' fails because reality therapy is the therapy built on personal responsibility for behaviour."}}, {"q": "\"Psychologists establish relationships of trust with those with whom they work\". This statement highlights the principle of:", "concept": "The answer is a: the statement is the opening line of Principle B, Fidelity and Responsibility, in the American Psychological Association's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct (2002, amended 2010 and 2016). The code has two layers: five aspirational General Principles (A to E) that guide judgement but are not enforced, and ten sections of enforceable Ethical Standards. The five principles are: A, Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (benefit clients and do no harm); B, Fidelity and Responsibility (build trust, keep commitments, accept responsibility for one's conduct, manage conflicts of interest and care about colleagues' ethical conduct); C, Integrity (accuracy, honesty and truthfulness, with no stealing, cheating or fraud); D, Justice (fairness and equal access to the benefits of psychology); and E, Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (privacy, confidentiality and self-determination). Fidelity means faithfulness: keeping promises and being someone others can rely on. Example: Sneha, a school counsellor, promises a teenage client that she will tell him before sharing anything with his parents, and keeps that promise; that is fidelity. Declining to become his private tutor as well, because a second role could exploit or confuse the relationship, is responsibility. Option a is right because 'establish relationships of trust' is the defining phrase of fidelity in the code. Exam trap: integrity also sounds like trustworthiness, but in the APA code it means honesty and accuracy in science, teaching and practice. Trust within working relationships belongs to fidelity.", "traps": {"b": "Principle D, Justice, asks psychologists to give all people fair and equal access to the benefits of psychology and equal quality of service, and to guard against bias. It is about fairness, not trust.", "c": "Principle C, Integrity, asks for accuracy, honesty and truthfulness in science, teaching and practice, and forbids fraud and deliberate misrepresentation. It is close in spirit, but the trust sentence belongs to Principle B.", "d": "'All of these' fails because the quoted sentence is the specific opening line of Principle B; Justice and Integrity are separate principles with their own wording."}}, {"q": "A counsellor must have ________ for the client.", "concept": "The answer is d: a counsellor must offer the client empathy, genuineness and positive regard, the three core conditions of person-centred therapy. In 'The necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change', Carl Rogers (1957) listed six conditions; the three that describe the therapist became the core conditions. Congruence or genuineness: the counsellor is real and transparent, with no professional front, so what they say matches what they feel; Rogers later called it the most basic. Unconditional positive regard: warm acceptance of the client as a person, without conditions of worth, even when the counsellor does not approve of every act; the term was coined by his student Stanley Standal (1954). Accurate empathic understanding: sensing the client's inner world 'as if' it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality, and communicating that understanding back. Rogers held that when a client experiences these conditions, the client's own actualising tendency does the rest. Example: Farhan tells his counsellor he copied in an exam. She does not lecture him (positive regard), reflects 'you feel ashamed, and also trapped by the pressure' (empathy), and admits honestly when she has not yet understood something (genuineness). Option d is right because each of the three is a required condition, and the options list all of them. Exam trap: students pick empathy alone because it is the best-known condition, but in Rogers's view all three must be present together.", "traps": {"a": "Accurate empathy is one core condition, but Rogers also required congruence and unconditional positive regard; empathy alone is incomplete.", "b": "Genuineness (congruence) is one core condition, the one Rogers called most basic, but it is not enough without empathy and positive regard.", "c": "Unconditional positive regard is one core condition; choosing it alone leaves out genuineness and empathy."}}, {"q": "The technique mainly encompassed in family therapy is:", "concept": "The answer is a: family therapy is mainly interpersonal, since it locates problems in the relationships between family members rather than only inside one person. Family therapy grew in the 1950s from systems thinking: Ludwig von Bertalanffy (1901 to 1972) argued in his general systems theory that a change in one part of a system changes the whole. Clinicians noticed that a patient who improved in hospital often relapsed at home, and that when one member got better another sometimes fell ill, so the family became the client. The member brought for help is the 'identified patient', a sign of a troubled system rather than its cause. The work is on interaction: Murray Bowen's family systems therapy (Family Therapy in Clinical Practice, 1978: differentiation of self, triangles), Salvador Minuchin's structural therapy (Families and Family Therapy, 1974: boundaries, subsystems), Jay Haley's strategic therapy (Problem-Solving Therapy, 1976: directives) and Virginia Satir's communication approach (Conjoint Family Therapy, 1964) all aim to change communication, roles, boundaries and alliances. Example: Ishaan, 12, refuses to go to school. The therapist sees that he and his mother are enmeshed while his father is left out, and works on the parents' alliance and the boundary around the child. Option a is right because family therapy works between people (interpersonal), not within one person's mind. Exam trap: group therapy also has several people in the room, so it looks right, but group members are usually strangers with no shared life outside the group. Key note: some texts call family therapy a special form of group therapy, and Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984) is a separate individual treatment for depression; the item contrasts an interpersonal focus with an intrapersonal one, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"b": "'Intrapersonal therapy' is not a named therapy. Intrapersonal means within one person (thoughts, conflicts, feelings), the opposite of family therapy's focus on relationships.", "c": "Group therapy (Irvin Yalom's therapeutic factors, 1970) treats several unrelated clients together, using the group as a social microcosm. Family therapy treats a natural system with a shared history, so its core technique is interpersonal work on that system rather than group work.", "d": "'None of these' fails because interpersonal work on communication, roles and boundaries is exactly what family therapy does."}}, {"q": "Frank Parsons, the proponent of vocational counselling, was educated as:", "concept": "The answer is b: Frank Parsons was educated as an engineer. Frank Parsons (1854 to 1908) entered Cornell University at 15 and graduated in civil engineering. He took a railway engineering post in Massachusetts, but lost it when the firm failed in the Panic of 1873. He then worked as a teacher, studied law and passed the Massachusetts bar (1881), and became a writer, lecturer and social reformer in Boston. Working with young people at the Civic Service House, he set up the Vocation Bureau of Boston in 1908, the first organised vocational guidance service. His book Choosing a Vocation appeared in 1909, after his death, and earned him the title father of vocational guidance. The book set out the trait-and-factor approach in three steps: a clear understanding of yourself (aptitudes, abilities, interests, limits), knowledge of the requirements and prospects of different kinds of work, and 'true reasoning' on the relation between the two. This matching idea runs through later career theory, including John Holland's person-environment fit (RIASEC, 1959 onwards). Option b is right because Parsons's formal training was in engineering, not in medicine or psychology; psychology as a profession barely existed when he was a student. Exam trap: because Parsons is a founding figure of counselling, students assume he was a psychologist. He was an engineer turned lawyer and social reformer.", "traps": {"a": "Parsons never studied medicine; after engineering he trained in law, so 'doctor' does not fit his education.", "c": "Parsons was not trained in psychology. He was at Cornell in the early 1870s, before Wilhelm Wundt opened his Leipzig laboratory (1879), and he built vocational guidance from practical work with young people.", "d": "Neuroscience became a named field only in the 1960s, long after Parsons died in 1908, and nothing in his training touched the brain."}}, {"q": "Who proposed the ERG theory of motivation?", "concept": "The answer is a: Clayton Alderfer proposed the ERG theory of motivation. Clayton Alderfer (1969; book Existence, Relatedness, and Growth, 1972) reworked Abraham Maslow's (1943) five-level hierarchy into three groups of needs. Existence needs are material and physical (food, water, pay, safe working conditions), matching Maslow's physiological and safety levels. Relatedness needs are for meaningful relationships with family, colleagues and friends, matching belongingness and the social part of esteem. Growth needs are for personal development and full use of one's abilities, matching self-esteem and self-actualisation. ERG differs from Maslow in three ways: it was tested on people at work, several needs can be active at the same time, and it adds frustration-regression, meaning that when a higher need is blocked a person falls back on a lower one and seeks it more strongly. Example: Sunil, a software engineer, is passed over for a promotion that would have let him lead a new project (growth blocked). He starts pushing hard for a pay rise and spends more time with his friends on the team (existence and relatedness). Option a is right because ERG, with its three needs and frustration-regression, is Alderfer's theory. Exam trap: Maslow is the natural bait because ERG is a compressed version of his hierarchy. But Maslow's model is taught as a ladder climbed from the bottom up, without frustration-regression; the three-letter ERG label belongs to Alderfer.", "traps": {"b": "Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed the five-level need hierarchy (physiological, safety, belongingness, esteem, self-actualisation) on which ERG is built; ERG itself, with three levels and frustration-regression, is Alderfer's.", "c": "Max Weber (1864 to 1920), the German sociologist, described bureaucracy and three types of authority (traditional, charismatic, rational-legal). He offered no theory of individual need motivation.", "d": "Daniel Goleman popularised emotional intelligence (Emotional Intelligence, 1995), the ability to understand and manage emotions in oneself and others. It is not a need theory of motivation."}}, {"q": "Who among the following is not an organizational Scientist?", "concept": "The answer is d: Ulric Neisser is not an organisational scientist; he was a cognitive psychologist. Ulric Neisser (1928 to 2012) named and shaped the field of cognitive psychology in his book Cognitive Psychology (1967), which drew together research on attention, pattern recognition and memory and gave us the terms iconic and echoic memory. In Cognition and Reality (1976) he proposed the perceptual cycle and pressed for ecologically valid research on memory in everyday life. None of this concerns how organisations are structured or managed. The other three are the founders of classical organisation theory. Frederick Winslow Taylor (The Principles of Scientific Management, 1911) used time-and-motion study to find the 'one best way' to do each job and tied pay to output. Henri Fayol (Administration Industrielle et Générale, 1916) described management from the top down: five functions (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling) and 14 principles such as unity of command and the scalar chain. Max Weber (1864 to 1920) described bureaucracy as the rational ideal of organisation: a hierarchy of offices, written rules, specialised roles, impersonal treatment and appointment on merit. Option d is right because Neisser's work lies in cognition, while Taylor, Fayol and Weber are the classical organisation theorists. Exam trap: Weber was a sociologist, not a manager or consultant, so students sometimes pick him as the odd one out; but his model of bureaucracy is a cornerstone of organisation theory.", "traps": {"a": "Frederick Winslow Taylor founded scientific management (1911), the first systematic study of work efficiency, so he is a core organisational scientist.", "b": "Henri Fayol, a French mining engineer and company director, founded administrative management theory (1916) with its five functions and 14 principles, so he is a core organisational scientist.", "c": "Max Weber, the German sociologist, gave organisation theory its model of bureaucracy and rational-legal authority, so he counts among the classical organisational theorists."}}, {"q": "The book \"Job Satisfaction: From Assessment to Intervention\" is authored by:", "concept": "The answer is c: Job Satisfaction: From Assessment to Intervention (Routledge, 2022) was written by Paul Spector. Paul Spector, an industrial and organisational psychologist and Distinguished Professor Emeritus at the University of South Florida, is a leading researcher on job satisfaction. He defines it simply as the extent to which people like or dislike their jobs, overall or facet by facet. In 1985 he published the Job Satisfaction Survey (JSS), a 36-item scale measuring nine facets: pay, promotion, supervision, fringe benefits, contingent rewards, operating procedures, co-workers, nature of work and communication. His book Job Satisfaction: Application, Assessment, Causes, and Consequences (1997) became a standard reference, and the 2022 book updates it. Its chapters cover the nature and assessment of satisfaction, how people feel about work, causes in the job and in the person (including personality and person-job fit), links with behaviour, performance and physical and mental health, and interventions to raise satisfaction. Option c is right because Spector is the author of this title, which grew out of his earlier book on the same subject. Exam trap: Frederick Herzberg is the name students link most with job satisfaction, through his two-factor theory, so he is the strongest bait; but his famous book is The Motivation to Work (1959), and he died in 2000, long before this title appeared.", "traps": {"a": "Frederick Herzberg proposed the two-factor (motivation-hygiene) theory in The Motivation to Work (1959, with Bernard Mausner and Barbara Snyderman): motivators create satisfaction, hygiene factors only prevent dissatisfaction. He did not write this 2022 book.", "b": "Abraham Maslow wrote 'A Theory of Human Motivation' (1943) and Motivation and Personality (1954), the source of the need hierarchy. He died in 1970, decades before this title.", "d": "Martin Seligman is known for learned helplessness and for launching positive psychology (1998); his books include Learned Optimism (1990) and Authentic Happiness (2002), not this one."}}, {"q": "The coach of a cricket team is to ______ leadership as _______ is to charismatic leadership.", "concept": "The answer is c: the coach of a cricket team is to transactional leadership as an inspirational leader is to charismatic leadership. James MacGregor Burns (Leadership, 1978) separated transactional leadership from transforming leadership, which Bernard Bass (1985) developed into the transformational model. Transactional leadership is an exchange: the leader sets clear targets and roles, rewards good performance (contingent reward) and corrects or sanctions poor performance (management by exception). A cricket coach fits the picture: fitness targets, selection as the reward for runs and wickets, being dropped for poor form. Charismatic leadership goes back to Max Weber's charismatic authority (Economy and Society, 1922), obedience to a person seen as extraordinary, and became a modern theory with Robert House (1977): the charismatic leader states an inspiring vision, shows great confidence and makes followers identify with the leader and the cause. An inspirational leader, such as Mahatma Gandhi leading the freedom movement, is the stock example. So the first blank pairs a role with the style that suits it (coach with transactional), and the second names the kind of person who embodies the given style (inspirational leader with charismatic). Option c is right because it is the only option in which both halves fit. Exam trap: option a tempts because politicians are often charismatic, but it makes the coach charismatic and leaves the analogy with no contrast between the two halves. Key note: many great coaches are also transformational or inspiring, so the analogy is loose; but only c gives a first term that suits a coach and a second that matches charismatic leadership, so the answer used here stands.", "traps": {"a": "Option a makes the coach charismatic and puts 'politician' in the second blank. A politician is just a role that may or may not be charismatic, and a coach's everyday target-and-reward style is not charismatic, so neither half fits well.", "b": "Transformational leadership (Burns, 1978; Bass, 1985) lifts followers' aims through vision and individual consideration, and 'manager' is a role tied more to transactional than to charismatic leadership, so the pairing is crossed.", "d": "Autocratic leadership is one of the climates studied by Kurt Lewin, Ronald Lippitt and Ralph White (1939), in which the leader decides alone; and 'charisma is to charismatic leadership' is circular, a quality and its own name, not an example of it."}}, {"q": "In terms of structure, organizations can be:", "concept": "The answer is d: organisations can be flat, tall or matrix in structure, so all three options are correct. Organisational structure is the formal pattern of jobs, reporting lines and authority, usually drawn as an organisation chart. Two features shape it: the number of levels in the hierarchy and the span of control, the number of people who report to one manager. A tall structure has many levels and a narrow span of control. It gives close supervision and clear promotion steps, but decisions are slow and messages get distorted as they pass up and down; armies and government departments are classic tall structures. A flat structure has few levels and a wide span of control. Decisions are faster, staff have more autonomy and communication is easy, but each manager carries a heavy load; start-ups and small firms are usually flat. A matrix structure lays project teams across functional departments, so each employee has two bosses, a functional manager (say, engineering) and a project manager (say, a new metro line). It pools expertise across projects but can cause conflict over priorities, and is common in engineering, consulting and aerospace firms. Example: Kavya, a designer at an engineering firm in Bengaluru, reports both to the head of design and to the manager of the bridge project she is placed on. Option d is right because each listed type is a recognised organisational structure. Exam trap: tall and flat are often taught as a pair, so students forget that the matrix design is a structure too. Other recognised forms include functional, divisional and network structures.", "traps": {"a": "A flat structure (few levels, wide span of control) is a real type, but choosing it alone leaves out tall and matrix structures, which are equally recognised.", "b": "A tall structure (many levels, narrow span of control) is a real type, but on its own it ignores flat and matrix designs.", "c": "The matrix structure (dual reporting to a functional and a project manager) is a real type, but picking it alone misses flat and tall structures."}}]});
